(Phnom Penh): In the Informal Meeting of the ASEAN Foreign Ministers, the 21st Meeting of the Political-Security Community Council and the 26th Meeting of the ASEAN Coordinating Council will be held on June 24, 2020. Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon, Minister of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation of Cambodia, outlined Cambodia's position on the South China Sea issue.
According to the head of the Cambodian diplomatic corps, in addition to the epidemic and socio-economic challenges, ASEAN is facing geopolitical difficulties due to the growing strategic rivalry of the superpowers. Despite the pressure on ASEAN, the ASEAN member states are not interested in electing a superpower and continue to support a multilateral system based on the rule of law.
The South China Sea has become a long-running territorial dispute between China and four ASEAN countries, including Indonesia, Malaysia, the Philippines and Vietnam. The conflict began to escalate when China began building artificial islands in the South China Sea.
The United States recently accused China of using the Kovid 19 crisis to cover up a campaign to boost sovereignty in the South China Sea by stepping up Chinese naval activity in the area.
While China has accused the United States of increasing its unprecedented actions in the Asia-Pacific region, this could lead to an eventual clash with the Chinese navy. Cambodian diplomat shares views on ASEAN Informal Meeting of Foreign Ministers via Video
(Phnom Penh): In the Informal Meeting of the ASEAN Foreign Ministers, the 21st Meeting of the Political-Security Community Council and the 26th Meeting of the ASEAN Coordinating Council will be held on June 24, 2020. Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon, Minister of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation of Cambodia, shared some views.
In these meetings, the Deputy Prime Minister stated the following:
* In addition to the epidemic and socio-economic challenges, ASEAN is facing geopolitical difficulties due to the growing strategic rivalry of superpowers. Despite the pressure on ASEAN, the ASEAN member states are not interested in electing a superpower and continue to support a multilateral system based on the rule of law.
As for the situation of the state of Rahang, Cambodia encourages the continuation of dialogue between stakeholders to ensure the safe, orderly and dignified repatriation process.
On the Korean Peninsula, Cambodia emphasizes the importance of ASEAN's role, especially through the ASEAN Regional Forum mechanism and other confidence-building measures to promote positive development on the Korean Peninsula. Cambodia appreciates the joint initiatives of Russia, the United States, China and Japan in developing an action plan.
On the issue of the South China Sea, Cambodia, as a non-sovereign state in the South China Sea, maintains neutrality in territorial disputes, and Cambodia hopes that the parties concerned should continue to maintain a favorable environment for maintaining peace, security and stability in the South China Sea. In the region that will contribute to the conclusion of code of conduct negotiations in the South China Sea.
* The Meeting noted the progress of the implementation of the ASEAN Security-Political Community Detailed Plan, in which 96% of the above activities were implemented. The meeting looked forward to the completion of the mid-term review report of the ASEAN Community Vision 2025, which includes a detailed plan of the ASEAN Political-Security Community 2025.
* The meeting discussed the preparations for the 36th ASEAN Summit and related meetings and approved the terms of reference of the ASEAN Fund for the Response to Kovid 19 and agreed on the need to accelerate the comprehensive efforts of ASEAN to Rehabilitate the Impact of Kovid 19
