Mr. Aing Serey Piseth stated that all the donations include: 1 wheelchair for the disabled, 25 kg of rice, 1 can of fish, 1 can of fish sauce, 1 can of soy sauce and 100,000 Riels. A Sanskrit inscription found at Tonle Snguot temple, Siem Reap province
(Siem Reap): Experts from the Apsara Authority found a Sanskrit inscription on a sandstone slab, which was used to decorate the front door of the central tower of the Tonle Snguot temple while cleaning the stone before preparing it. Assemble at original location.
The inscription was kept by the APSARA Authority at the headquarters of the Department of Temple Preservation in Angkor Park and Preventive Archeology on the morning of June 24, 2020.
According to Mr. M. Sokrithy, Director of the Department of Preservation of Temples in Angkor Park and Preventive Archeology, the inscription may not belong to Tonle Snguot Hospital, perhaps from a temple used as a stone. The floor is laid face down with the inscription on the bottom. As for the background, there are no letters, they are turned upside down and used as cascades for supporting the door beams.
This case has lost part of the character, but there is still a lot that can be studied further, and this is not considered a case of destruction, because they cut the space to be a floor stone in the construction of the dry river temple. Classic. Taking old stones from another place to build a new temple is also a common practice in ancient times.
The uniqueness of this inscription is that it has not been studied yet, written in Sanskrit, it has 55 lines, 100 cm high, 40 cm wide and 20 cm thick. According to preliminary conclusions on the character of the cat and the details, which are comparable to the inscriptions at Phnom Dey, located north of the Tonle Snguot temple, it can be assumed that the inscriptions date to the end of the 11th century AD. Early 12th century AD.
