The company started construction of the new airport on March 15, 2020 and is scheduled to finish on March 14, 2023, with a total construction period of 36 months (3 years). Mr. Net Pheaktra: Cambodia has recovered under the careful preservation of stakeholders!
This is according to Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State and spokesman for the Ministry of Environment, confirmed to the media this morning, June 24, 2020, on the occasion that he received two turtles from the people. Purchased from fishermen in Koh Kong province to be handed over to the Ministry and WSC to be released back into the nature reserve.
A spokesman for the Ministry of Environment said: "Cambodia has been increasing the number of tortoises or fibrous turtles through the WCS Conservation Project to increase the tortoise population." "Because this turtle represents our Cambodian nation."
Mr. Neth Pheaktra continued that the conservation and conservation of biodiversity, especially rare wildlife species, has made Cambodia richer, especially by protecting this rare species, and only then can Cambodia attract the economy through eco-tourism. .
Mr. Neth Pheaktra said that Cambodia is recognized as rich in biodiversity and some of the most endangered species in the world are present in Cambodia as a species of royal turtle. Neighboring Cambodia, such as Thailand and Vietnam, are extinct.
A senior official at the Ministry of Environment said that from 2002 to 2020, there were 43 nests of turtles, 678 eggs and 450 hatchlings. In particular, WSC officials organized the rearing of the turtles at a reptile conservation center in Koh Kong province. In particular, from 2015 to 2020, the organization released 86 turtles in natural canals.
According to Som Sitha, director of the Royal Turtle Conservation Project of the Cambodian Wildlife Conservation Society (WCS), the royal turtle has been sent to a reptile rescue and conservation center in Sre Ambel district, Koh Kong province, since 2006.
The expert concluded that the two turtles were about 14 years old, while their lifespan is estimated to be more than 100 years. According to him, the female can be up to 60 cm in size and can reproduce at the age of 20, while the male can be up to 49 cm.
The king turtle was thought to be extinct in Cambodia until 2000, when the Fisheries Administration and WCS conducted a study and found a small number of its remains in the Sre Ambel canal system in Cambodia.
WCS officials say 450 baby turtles are currently being raised at the Sre Ambel local reptile rescue and conservation center, while 86 juvenile turtles have been released. To live naturally.
The Royal Turtle, a reptile on the International Union for Conservation of Nature (IUCN) Red List, is among the 25 most endangered species in the world.
This rare species of turtle is threatened by deforestation, flooding, illegal fishing, such as the use of fishing gear, fishing nets, prohibited fishing in the restricted area, etc., especially dredging has destroyed the habitat for royal turtles. Spawning, which greatly reduces the number of turtles, which could eventually become extinct if not properly protected.
According to the documents, the royal turtle is a species of animal that is considered as a symbol of animals in Cambodia through the recognition by Royal Decree No. NS / RKOT / 0305/149 dated March 21. 2005.
