Mr. Neth Pheaktra: Improving the livelihood of the people around the protected area will be able to cut down on wildlife hunting and natural crimes
(Phnom Penh): Improving the livelihood of people in the communities around protected areas and promoting legal education as well as the benefits of conserving natural resources and animals will be able to cut down on wildlife hunting and natural resource crime.
This was stated by Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State and Spokesperson of the Ministry of Environment on June 24, 2020, on the occasion of receiving two royal turtles from the people at the Ministry of Environment.
However, Mr. Neth Pheaktra said that the problem now is that people continue to sneak into the forest with embedded traps, and those traps are more modern, using traps in the forest across the road. Animals come and go.
"If there is no market demand, if people stop eating wild animals, I believe that some hunters will not go hunting, but in some restaurants, some places, especially in eco-tourism, "Secretly selling meat as food."
Mr. Neth Pheaktra expressed his excitement that Cambodia is very proud to be recognized by the world as a country rich in biodiversity, where many rare species are present in Cambodia, such as the royal tortoise. Cranes, vultures, squirrels or peacocks, as well as other rare animals.
On behalf of the Ministry of Environment and Minister Say Sam Al, he called on all people not to eat wild animals, to report wildlife crimes and encourage all people to participate in protection through wildlife trade. Original.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra added that for the perpetrators of wildlife crime, Cambodia already has this law, especially to further strengthen the illegal wildlife perpetrators, in addition, the Ministry will strive to educate the law to the people. Basically.
From January to April 2020, 1,260 park rangers from the Ministry of Environment patrolled 7,078 times in 60 protected areas and biodiversity corridors covering a total area of 7.2 million hectares. Occasionally, there were 2,333 patrols from protected areas recognized by the Ministry of Environment and natural resource crime.
In the four months of natural resource crime prevention, park officials and the protected area community confiscated 970 chainsaws, 53 cars, tractors, and 126 motorbikes as a means of committing crimes. Detained 203 bulldozers and removed 8,950 wildlife traps in disguise, and confiscated 239 firearms used for hunting.
