There are 20 diamond-producing countries, with South Africa currently the fifth-largest diamond producer after Australia, Congo, Bosnia-Herzegovina and Russia.
Although diamonds are the hardest material in the world, during August 2006, German scientists developed something even harder than diamonds.
The material was produced in a laboratory called a tonado-carbon, which was pressed and heated to a temperature of 2,226 degrees Celsius on the hardest carbon molecule.
The above material is so hard that you can scratch the diamond without exerting too much force.
