Please be informed that "Labor Cash Project for Rural Infrastructure Development and Farmer Support Phase 2 for 2020-2022" worth $ 100 million was officially announced by Dr. Aun Porn Moniroth, Deputy Prime Minister and Minister of Economy and Finance on June 11, 2020
The "Labor Cash Project for Rural Infrastructure Development and Farmers Support" aims to contribute to poverty reduction, improve the living standards and well-being of rural people and poor farmers through:
1: Creating short-term job opportunities through the cash flow program that will help solve the short-term emergency life, as well as create capital for further investment in agriculture or other suitable occupations.
2: Development of agricultural and rural infrastructure, including: roads, canals for irrigation, and low-cost electricity connection to agricultural pumping stations.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen: Cambodia is boosting the agricultural sector as rice exports increase and claim that Cambodia has no food insecurity (Video inside) (Phnom Penh): Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia on 24 This June 2020 highlighted that Cambodia is promoting the agricultural sector as rice exports have increased significantly and claimed that despite Cambodia, Cambodia has no food insecurity.
This emphasis was made on the occasion of Samdech Techo Hun Sen announcing the implementation of the program to provide cash to the poor and vulnerable people with Kovid 19 disease at about 600,000 families at the Peace Palace this morning, June 24, 2020. .
Samdech Techo Hun Sen stated, "We are promoting the agricultural sector, which this year, due to Kovid-19, our rice exports have increased significantly, before we exported about 500,000 tons. In a year, now this year may reach 800,000 tons or more.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen emphasized, "Despite Cambodia, one of the strengths of Cambodia is that every year we have about 6 million tons of rice." "Some say Cambodia is vulnerable to food insecurity, but just confirm that we have millions of hectares of arable land."
At the same time, the Prime Minister of Cambodia also said that so far, Cambodia's rain-fed rice has already reached more than 1.5 million hectares, and about this coming week, especially in July is usually a month. Harvest light rice to continue in another season.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen went on to claim that Cambodia is insecure, instead Cambodia will have a shared responsibility with the international community in selling food abroad.
It should be noted that according to the calculations of the Cambodian Rice Federation, Cambodia's rice exports in the first five months of 2020 earned more than 241 million dollars.
China controls 38% of Cambodia's export market. Next are 34% in Europe, 13% in ASEAN and 15% in Australia, the United States and the United States.
Although Cambodian rice is subject to an import tax of 150 euros per tonne, export growth to the EU is growing at more than 51%. France is the largest importer with over 45,000 tons.
For the May 2020 market, the European Union exported more than 24,000 tonnes, or 44% of Cambodia's export market share, most of which were almost 100% fragrant rice, organic rice and steamed rice. It also exported more than 14,000 tons to China, or 26% of Cambodia's rice export market share.
The average growth in exports was due to a temporary suspension of white rice exports, but growth in China was similar to that of the European Union and will start next month. Growth in exports to other markets such as Gabon, Australia, Russia and New Zealand was also impressive.
Ministry of Environment receives two turtles weighing nearly 40 kg from villagers for release in conservation
The handover ceremony was held in the presence of Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State and Spokesperson of the Ministry of Environment, a representative of the WSC and the people who own the turtles at the Ministry of Environment.
According to Mr. Pheaktra, the two turtles were handed over to the Wildlife Conservation Society of Cambodia (WCS) for conservation in the sanctuary.
At the same time, Minister Say Samal, Minister of Environment, praised the volunteer by donating their own money to participate in the care and conservation of this rare species of turtle family of Ms. Lay Phon Theng.
A senior official at the Ministry of Environment confirmed that stopping people from eating wildlife would end poaching and trafficking.
Lay Phon Theng, who donated the rare turtle species to the Ministry of Environment, said she and her family bought the two turtles from a fisherman for $ 400 each during a trip to Koh Kong province last year. This past weekend.
Speaking to reporters at the handover ceremony, the resident, who now lives in Phnom Penh, confirmed that the two rare turtles had been persuaded by fishermen to sell them at a restaurant in the area. Sre Ambel District, Koh Kong Province.
The expert concluded that the two turtles were about 14 years old, while their lifespan is estimated to be more than 100 years. He said that female tortoises can grow up to 60 centimeters in size and can reproduce at the age of 20, while males can grow up to 49 centimeters.
This rare species of turtle is threatened by deforestation, flooding, illegal fishing, such as the use of fishing gear, fishing nets, prohibited fishing in the restricted area, etc., especially dredging has destroyed the habitat for royal turtles. Spawning, which greatly reduces the number of turtles, which could eventually become extinct if not properly protected.
