Meanwhile, Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State and Spokesperson of the Ministry of Environment, led a delegation from the Ministry of Environment, provincial authorities, chefs from the Le Royal Hotel and a group of journalists on a three-day mission from the 18th to the 20th. June 2020 in this Peam Krasop community to learn about life, fishing, sea and visit the duck beak farm to help find a market for the community.
The community of Peam Krasop Nature Reserve is located in Bang Kayak Village, Peam Krasop Commune, Mondul Seima District, Koh Kong Province, about 7 km from Royal Khemarak City. All international tourists can travel to this area in just 20 to 25 minutes.
Through face-to-face meetings with senior officials of the Ministry of Environment, the community expressed their unimaginable excitement to see the relevant ministries reach out to them in person to find out about their living conditions, daily livelihoods and study. Their difficulties.
According to Mr. Phorn Chamnang, a Peam Krasop community member, after being educated about the benefits of mangrove trees, all communities have stopped cutting and planting mangrove trees to increase their beauty. Sea forests, which are home to many fish species, especially provide plankton as food for marine animals and attract tourists to visit Peam Krasop community, he is increasing day by day.
At the same time, Mr. Phorn Chamnan also expressed concern about the lack of poles for raising duck beak because people in the community stopped planting trees as well as mangrove trees to be raised poles by ordering from other areas. Purchasing this pole has caused a lot of difficulties due to the location of the order is far from the community and it is more expensive than before, and the trees that feed this cluster include: dependent trees, sugarcane and mountain trees, etc.
He said the duck beak market was almost entirely dependent on traders from Thailand, and traders from Vietnam have only been around for a few years.
At the same time, Mr. Phorn Chamnay also expressed his happiness after meeting directly with the leaders of the Ministry of Environment and the leaders of Koh Kong province, the chefs from the Le Royal Hotel now. He said that if hotels or restaurants in the country are interested in ordering shellfish from them, he will try to expand the shellfish farming to meet market demand.
The community, which has been raising duck beak for more than 10 years, added that he personally raised 4,000 duck beaks and was able to harvest more than 500,000 tons per year. In the whole community, there are 350 families raising duck beak.
According to Mr. Neth Pheaktra, the mangrove forest is a source of income for the ecotourism community, and in addition it provides potential for biodiversity and, in particular, we can sell more carbon, which does not include providing On other economic sectors as well.
According to the senior official of the Ministry of Environment, the Peam Krasop community is rich in mangrove forests, and in the four coastal provinces of Koh Kong, there are a total of 46,000 hectares of mangrove forest and wildlife sanctuary area. This embankment is also surrounded by mangrove forests. In this area, we have the Peam Krasop resort, through which we see a lot of national and international tourists.
Through the mangrove forest, which is part of attracting tourists and especially providing great biodiversity potential, the Secretary of State stated that in the past, mangrove forests were cut down for charcoal and daily use, but now people Many, especially fishermen, are aware of its benefits and have taken care to protect it carefully and plan for reforestation.
Each year, the community can collect at least 10,000 tons of beaked shells, and the price is from 1,000 riel to 3,000 riel, depending on the type of shells are good or not so good. Legal Awareness: Defamation of Corpses and Insulting Cemeteries
Although in everyday life there are few abuses of corpses and cemeteries, the law protects and does not allow criminals to take advantage of any immoral or immoral acts. That.
To better understand what acts are considered to be detrimental to the integrity of the body and insult to the cemetery? And to what extent will those who commit such acts face punishment? Fresh News Legal Awareness Program in collaboration with the Cambodian Lawyers for International Cooperation will present and share to the following students:
In Cambodia, in 2014, there was a case where a suspect stole the coffin of a 17-year-old girl who had just died to rape her body. Such defilement and immorality are also considered by law to be detrimental to the integrity of the body.
In the past, we have seen acts that are considered insulting to the cemetery, including digging graves to steal valuables or artifacts that the family of the deceased buried with the corpse. In addition, there are some other inappropriate activities such as vandalism of the cemetery without permission for any inappropriate purpose or immoral acts in the form of insulting the cemetery.
In summary, the offense of infringing on the integrity of the body and insulting the cemetery is a misdemeanor that identifies and punishes the person who committed immoral acts and abused the body and insulted the cemetery. In order to escape these criminal penalties, it is essential that citizens understand more about what to do and what to avoid.
