Myanmar plans to reopen tourism in Q3, with flights to Cambodia, Laos, Vietnam and Thailand to be launched first
Rangoon: Myanmar expects to be able to resume domestic tourism in the third quarter of 2020, and for the first step to help the tourism sector recover under the tourism relief plan in Yangon. COVID-19 session released. This was stated by officials of the Ministry of Tourism and Myanmar Hotels, quoted by the Xinhua News Agency on Saturday evening, June 20, 2020.
Currently, the Ministry of Tourism and the Ministry of Religious Affairs and Culture are discussing the reopening of pagodas and cultural sites as soon as possible. Meanwhile, regional tourism will resume by the fourth quarter of this year, focusing on the reconnection of flights between Cambodia, Laos, Vietnam and Thailand.
Myanmar also expects Asian tourism, especially with China and Japan, to resume in early 2021, with hopes of recovering between 50-60%, while international tourism is expected to pick up. Between 80-90 percent at the beginning of 2022.
The tourism recovery plan has three main strategies: survival, financial independence, and emergency budgeting. In addition, easing of restrictions on re-investment in Myanmar tourism and easing of travel regulations will be implemented in accordance with the timelines set out above.
It should be noted that under the above scheme, the relevant ministries of Myanmar will start to take measures such as tax exemption for one year license, extension of state hotel rent for 6 months, online training, daily allowance and package. Funding for related entities, launching digital tourism promotion campaigns and local brand promotion strategies through e-commerce.
