General knowledge: Confucius spent the rest of his life on the hunt to educate people around the world for thousands of years to this day.
The life story of this Chinese philosopher is not very comfortable, he was persecuted almost to death by traitorous officials, but he was still able to escape from the mermaid's fangs by various means. Use the rest of this life to spread education throughout the ancient Chinese world.
What kind of person was Confucius, and what kind of storm did he face before he became a famous philosopher to this day? Did Confucius realize that he was considered by the next generation to be a great nobleman?
Here is a true biography of the Chinese philosopher Confucius:
He later got a job as a clerk and registrar in the city of Jufu, the capital of the Luo kingdom, but despite his good work, the consular officer did not give up further studies.
According to ancient Chinese tradition, when a parent or relative dies, he or she takes a break from work to mourn the dead. Concerns mourn his mother for three years, living in a house. Quiet, isolated. During that time he spent all his time studying philosophy. He was interested in morality and virtue and decided to live by following the principles related to these dharmas.
Through two students who have a good relationship with the Royal Palace, the Fukushima always visits and spends a lot of time reading books at the Royal Library. He also learned the famous types of music that were performed at the Chefu Palace, which was when he met Lao Xu, the founder of Taoism or Taoism. He was greatly influenced by Mr. Lao and spent a lot of time studying with Mr. Lao Xer.
Connor taught his students the principles of good behavior and good governance. His teachings are based on morality rather than religion. He taught people to live with dignity and caution. Kong is revered and considered a wise man by the Chinese called Ji (meaning abbot).
Confucius lived in a feudal system in which land was allocated only for warlords, while morals and social governance were degraded. He is trying to figure out how to restore social balance. He understands that change can only happen if those leaders understand his ancient traditions and philosophy. It is necessary to change them to believe in ancient traditions.
Confucius had a passion for music and traditional ceremonies, believing that the ceremonies symbolized social control and that music entertained our minds.
Under such a powerful system of government, the Lu kingdom grew and became an important kingdom. Neighboring kingdoms were concerned about the influence of Confucius, and they devised a plan to oust him as justice official. They began to provoke the king of the kingdom of Lou to break his heart and began to ignore the royal work, thinking only of having fun with a woman. Dissatisfied with the king's actions, Confucius also resigned as top justice official.
Confucius compiled his ancient ideals into five books, entitled "The Five Ancient Books," which recorded his teachings and biographies. This record is based on Chinese history during the Luo Dynasty from 722-481 BC. He wanted his apostles to consider themselves and study the outside world through the ancient Scriptures, rather than formulate theories of life.
Confucius died in 479 BC, and his body was buried in a tomb in present-day Yuefu, Shandong Province.
