The invasion is considered to be the first military action during the Cold War. In July, US troops also joined the Korean War to help South Korea, and for the United States, it was a direct war against the communist regime. After fighting each other across the 38th parallel, the fighting came to a standstill and there were heavy casualties on both sides.
At the time, the United States was working hard to reach a ceasefire with North Korea. Otherwise, they feared a large-scale war with the Soviet Union and China, and some warned of a third world war. Finally, in July 1953, the "Korean War" came to an end through a ceasefire, not a formal treaty to end the war.
In total, millions of soldiers and innocent people lost their lives in this war, which most people in the United States consider a "forgotten war" because it is less popular than other wars. Such as World War I, World War II and the Vietnam War. The Korean Peninsula has been divided to this day.
South and North Korea The Korean Peninsula has been under Japanese rule since the early 20th century, and after World War II, the fate of the peninsula fell into the hands of the United States and the Soviet Union. You decide. The two sides agreed to temporarily divide the Korean Peninsula into two, with the 38th parallel being the unofficial border. By that division, the United States controlled the south and the Soviets controlled the north.
The two Korean leaders are still uneasy, as they are all patriots who want to reunite Korea into one country, but have different tendencies, one following in the West and one following in communism. Conflicts on the 38th parallel were frequent, with a total of nearly 10,000 Korean troops killed on both sides before the war officially broke out. Inevitably, North Korea also raised troops to invade South Korea in 1950.
Cold War and the Korean War to the United States North Korea's invasion of South Korea was not just a normal internal conflict between the two Koreas, but could be the first step in the communist regime's campaign to rule the world. The whole world. For this reason, the United States understands that non-intervention is not absolute.
The then US President, Harry Truman, said that without South Korea, the Soviet Union would not stop at Korea and continue to expand its communist influence around the world. Therefore, the war on the Korean Peninsula was a symbol of the rivalry between the liberal and communist regimes around the world during the Cold War.
When North Korean troops invaded the South Korean capital, Seoul, the United States was preparing its troops to wage war against the communist regime, not just North Korea under the auspices of the United Nations. At first, it was just a deterrent to North Korea withdrawing from South Korea, and it was not easy for South Korea and the United States allies.
In the first step, the goal setting of this new strategy was remarkably successful. The Battle of Incheon pushed North Korean troops out of Seoul and back to the other side of the 38th parallel. The United States and South Korea also crossed the line and continued to strike the North Korean capital, Pyongyang.
But as Allied troops approached the Yalu River, the border between North Korea and China, China began to worry that if North Korea was captured by the United States, it would be the biggest threat at its doorstep. Body. Therefore, Chinese leader Mao Zedong sent troops to help North Korea and warned the United States to stay away from the Yalu River if a large-scale war did not break out.
A large-scale war with China was also something Truman did not want, as it would give the Soviets an opportunity to expand their influence in Europe, and because China was an ally of the Soviets, the Soviets could intervene to the point of using nuclear weapons. Put together, which will cause millions of deaths. But for US commander MacArthur, he disagreed with his president.
The Korean War reached a stalemate, and in July 1951, Truman and his new commander (McArthur were ousted a few months earlier for disobedience) began negotiations. With China and North Korea at Phan Mon Chum village. However, fighting continued along the parallel line due to the lack of negotiations.
The death toll in the Korean War The fighting in the Korean War did not last long, but the bloodshed was intense, with a total of nearly 5 million people lost their lives, more than half of them innocent civilians. Korea.
Although the Korean Peninsula is now peaceful, technically South and North Korea are still at war with each other, as the two countries have no formal end-of-war treaty, only a ceasefire. South Korea is now under President Moon Jae-in, while North Korea is under Kim Jong-un, the grandson of Kim Il-sung.
