Exhibition of historical times, the journey towards the overthrow of the genocidal regime of Pol Pot by Samdech Techo Hun Sen, June 20, 1977
(Phnom Penh): In commemoration of the 43rd Anniversary of the Day of Remembrance of Samdech Techo Hun Sen's Journey to the Overthrow of the Pol Pot Genocidal Regime, Held on June 20, 2020 A historical time with 107 pictures, 36 paintings and 6 maps in the "Koh Thmor Documentation Center".
The Koh Thmor Document Center is the brainchild of General Nem Sovath, Special Adviser to Samdech Pich Sena Tea Banh and Director General of the General Department of Politics and Foreign Affairs of the Ministry of National Defense, with Mr. Chhang Youk, Director of the Documentation Center of Cambodia, on a field trip to the region. Koh Thmor on March 25, 2020.
Samdech Techo was interrogated by the Vietnamese side for the first time there. Samdech Hun Sen was then sent to Minh Phuoc district, where he was interrogated again by district cadres. In 1978, Samdech Hun Sen became a founding member of the United Front for National Salvation of Cambodia. With the cooperation of various patriotic movements and the support of the Vietnamese Volunteer Forces, the Front saved the country and the entire Cambodian people on January 7, 1979.
A painting of Samdech Techo's journey towards the overthrow of the genocidal regime of Pol Pot by artist Uth Roeun. Uth Roeun first started painting in the 1960s. He published a comic book about Preah Thong Neang Neak, Thananychey and Tum Teav. During the Khmer Rouge regime, he was required by the Khmer Rouge to paint a "construction plan for a revolutionary organization." Mr. Uth Roeun retired in 2001. He is currently an artist at the Victory Monument.
Dinh Fong and Ho Van Tay were the first former filmmakers to make the documentary "Tuol Sleng Prison" on January 10, 1979. They entered Tuol Sleng Prison through the stench of corpses and took several photos of the prison scene, as well as rescuing five orphans whose parents had been killed by the Khmer Rouge. Today, Ho Van Tay lives in Binh Thanh district.
In the early 1980s, the victims' skulls, torso and limbs were collected in stupas or shrines on the grounds of a pagoda near the scene of the massacre as evidence and for traditional rites. Religion. On May 20, every year, Cambodians across the country hold a memorial service in honor of those who died during the Khmer Rouge regime.
The photo exhibition shows the image of Samdech Hun Sen, who began to think about the policy of winning, the photo of Prime Minister Hun Sen speaking at the National Assembly in the 1990s. Samdech's discussions focused on initiating a win-win policy that led to the peaceful and peaceful reunification of the entire Khmer Rouge and the Cambodian government in 1998.
