Please read the decision below: The Ministry of National Defense celebrates the 43rd anniversary of the Day of Remembrance of the Road to National Liberation from the Pol Pot Genocidal Regime
(Phnom Penh): On June 20, 2020, the Ministry of National Defense will hold a ceremony to commemorate the 43rd anniversary of the day Samdech Techo Hun Sen and his comrades left Cambodia in search of national liberation from the genocidal regime. Pol Pot race.
The ceremony will take place at the historical site of the starting point of the struggle in Koh Thmor village, Tonlong commune, Memot district, Tbong province. The ceremony will be presided over by Samdech Pich Sena Tea Banh, Deputy Prime Minister and Minister of Defense, and will be attended by Vietnamese Ambassadors to Cambodia as well as military attachés of various countries in Cambodia.
It should be noted that on June 20, 2020, tomorrow is the 43rd anniversary period (June 20, 1977 - June 20, 2020) that the 25-year-old Hun Sen fighter and 4 other loyal fighters decided to The most tense situation left the land, the departed wife and beloved children crossed the most dangerous border to Vietnam to seek support for the liberation of the nation and the people of Cambodia from the cruel clutches of Pol Pot.
The locations where Comrade Hun Sen and his group crossed from the Cambodian side were at Trapaing Roka, Trapeang Khor, and Ang Dach, with a large, tall tree nearby. Opposite is the district of Loc Ninh in Vietnam's Song Ber province. Before entering Vietnam, Comrade Hun Sen turned to Cambodia with tears in his eyes and said in his heart, "The killer."
At 2:00 AM on June 21, 1977, Comrade Hun Sen walked about 200 meters into Vietnamese territory. Everyone rested until dawn to set their sights on the journey and had nothing to eat.
That was the most stressful time of all for the four people who came with him, thinking that they could take Comrade Hun Sen to death or go somewhere unknown if they did not return. All four of them felt anxious, even though they were tired, could not sleep, could not breathe and waited until Comrade Hun Sen returned at more than midnight on June 22, 1977.
On June 22, 1977, Comrade Hun Sen and his four passengers left for Songberg by truck. Vietnam increasingly recognized that Comrade Hun Sen had a high rank as a brigade commander and was not a group sent by Pol Pot to raid Vietnam.
From Koh Thmor, the night of June 20, 1977 became an important day in the history of Cambodia, when Comrade Hun Sen began his journey towards the overthrow of the genocidal regime of Pol Pot.
