(Koh Kong): "Mangrove forest is a strategic corridor connecting ecosystems from the mainland, coasts and seas, and also a barrier against natural disasters and climate change, not only contributing to national economic development and reducing "Poverty plays an important role as a storehouse, a habitat and a source of natural fish, a rich source of marine resources, and we need to protect, conserve and replant mangroves."
This was stated by Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State and Spokesman of the Ministry of Environment on June 18, 2020, on the occasion that he led a delegation of the Ministry of Environment to inspect the process of raising snails, ducks and other products in Peam Krasop Protected Area Community of Peam Krasop Wildlife Sanctuary in Koh Kong Province.
Koh Kong province, according to the Secretary of State of the Ministry of Environment, has the largest number of mangrove forests among Cambodia's four coastal provinces.
He said the conservation of mangroves and related ecosystems has been identified as a natural adaptation strategy and measures to mitigate the effects of climate change. Mangroves are a buffer zone between land and sea, counteracting the serious problems of coastal erosion and the threat of sea level rise. Mangrove forests help control the inflow of seawater into natural soils.
Recognizing the importance of mangroves, the Secretary of State for the Ministry of Environment called on the people, especially those in protected areas, to participate in the protection and conservation of mangrove trees and to plant more mangrove trees for the sustainability of the environment and marine biodiversity. Tomorrow morning, the Royal Academy of Cambodia opens a discussion on Cambodia's preparations for economic recovery after the end of Kovid 19
(Phnom Penh): On the morning of Friday, June 19, 2020, tomorrow, the Royal Academy of Cambodia will hold a roundtable discussion on the readiness of Cambodia to prepare for economic recovery after the end of the Kovid 19 epidemic.
Economics and Labor experts from the Ministry of Labor, Ministry of Commerce and the Royal Academy of Cambodia will join the forum to explain and demonstrate the measures that will be taken to revive Cambodia's economy after the end of Kovid 19. The planet.
Cambodia, after the end of the war in 1998, has experienced remarkable economic growth, growing at around 7% annually over the last 20 years.
In particular, the Asian Development Bank forecasts that the Cambodian economy will shrink by more than 2 percent by 2020. However, the Royal Government has forecast that the Cambodian economy will recover by about 3.5 percent by 2021.
