(Phnom Penh): Many events that have been happening in the world have given us many thoughtful thoughts, one of the most important of which is freedom and well-being of human society, or worse. Which is more important, freedom or life?
What makes us even more enlightened now is the management of the 19-crisis crisis and the crisis of racism that has escalated into violence, turning major US cities into battlefields. A lot that looks no different from a Hollywood movie.
The disadvantage of excessive freedom is destroying the well-being of the society and is also pushing the liberal society, especially the United States, into a deep crisis, and the most shocking thing is that hundreds of thousands of people have died and continue to die. Every day.
* Hong Kong in prehistory and history
To reduce the trade imbalance with China, Britain imported large quantities of Indian-grown opium on Chinese soil, making more and more Chinese people addicted to it. Chheng also took fierce action against the British to stop the opium trade.
During the war between the People's Republic of China, Zhang Kai-shek and the People's Republic of China of Mao Zedong forced many Chinese to flee to Hong Kong. With such a large population, Hong Kong became the first Asian tiger economy since the 1950s. With a rapidly growing population, colonial governors began reforms to improve infrastructure and public services.
At midnight on July 1, 1997, Hong Kong returned to Chinese rule after a century and a half (more than 150 years) of British colonial rule. The return of Hong Kong territory to China aims to establish "one country, two regimes" relations between China and Hong Kong.
Even the United States agrees with China's policy, with the two powers declaring Hong Kong to be China's special administrative region for 50 years on July 1, 1997. Hong Kong will continue to operate in a capitalist economy and people will have the right to freedom and religion at least until 2047.
* Hong Kong flag
The Hong Kong flag has been created and changed six times, including:
The Hong Kong flag under British rule was first created in 1871 and was in use until 1876.
The sixth, with a red surface and white flowers in the center, was established in 1997 and is still in use today.
