(Phnom Penh): Dr. Mom Kong, Executive Director of the Cambodian Movement for Health, received a special award from the Secretary-General of the World Health Organization for his efforts and achievements in controlling tobacco products in Cambodia to protect the well-being of the people.
Dr. Li Ailan, WHO Representative in Cambodia, said that for more than 20 years in the fight against tobacco in Cambodia, Dr. has worked hard to advocate and campaign with relevant media, youth media. Women, teachers, monks and tobacco victims in pushing for a law on the control of tobacco products in Cambodia, which was passed in 2015.
She said the program was officially announced after being selected by the World Health Organization (WHO) in Cambodia, supported by the World Health Organization (WHO) Asia-Pacific Office Director, before final approval by the Secretary-General of the World Health Organization.
"We are proud that our counterparts have been recognized by the Secretary-General of the World Health Organization as champions in the field of tobacco control," she said. "As we know, one of the main obstacles in developing and enforcing tobacco control laws is the interference of the tobacco industry."
In response to this, the campaign "It's time! Stop cheating tobacco companies ”was created to inspire young people to participate in preventing the machinations of the tobacco industry.
At present, Dr. Mom Kong has been leading the Cambodian Movement for Health to continue its willingness to control tobacco products in Cambodia and to reduce the risk to the lives and economy of Cambodians caused by consumption. Tobacco.
The doctor monitors and monitors the implementation of the law on tobacco control on a regular basis and reports to the relevant authorities and local authorities for action.
It should be noted that according to the results of the research study on investment in tobacco control in Cambodia in 2019 of the United Nations Development Program, the World Health Organization and the Secretariat of the Convention on the Control of Tobacco Products. Tobacco use kills 15,000 Cambodians every year, with 33% of all deaths occurring in the lowest-income population.
In addition, tobacco use caused a loss of 649 million US dollars, equivalent to 3% of Cambodia's gross domestic product (GDP).
