Land dispute between siblings for more than 10 years has been resolved according to the principle of win-win Samdech Techo Prime Minister
The long-running land dispute, which lasted for more than 10 years, separated Kem Sokun from her sister, Kem Bophal, and nephew, Yav Sopheavuth, who now has a career as a lawyer. This happened between 1990-1992 when Kem Sokun went to work as the third secretary in the Czech Republic. At that time, she sent some money to her brother to buy some land in Cambodia.
But the lands are only the names of sisters and nephews, and there is no name of the grandmother who wanted to sell some things but could not do so, leading to a lawsuit for more than 10 years, which has not yielded any results other than The more one hates each other, the older one gets.
Then, on the morning of June 15, 2020, Mr. Duong Dara invited the three parties to mediate and reach a settlement, which reached an agreement by all parties to end the dispute today, in which both parties He signed the agreement to end the dispute and agreed to withdraw all complaints from the courts and all institutions and promised not to quarrel and not to sue each other.
Mrs. Kem Sokun, Mrs. Kem Bophal and Mr. Yav Sopheavuth expressed their deepest gratitude to the Prime Minister of Cambodia, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, for providing a peaceful solution based on the principle of win-win. Everyone wins together. Mrs. Kem Sokun's family also thanked Mr. Duong Dara for helping to resolve this dispute and get the results that everyone can accept satisfactorily.
