(Kampong Thom): After a few rains at the beginning of this rainy season, the Sambor Prei Kuk temple area seems to have received blessings from Preah Phirun, which is the time to perform the ritual of pointing the rain to irrigate the forest in the protected area around the ancient Khmer capital to grow. Fresh young green leaves, flowers, colorful flowers, eel poet eyes.
This kind of view has beautified the whole resort to be more beautiful, not to mention the words of admiration of those who have arrived there. Walking through the shade of the cool trees, the temple stands smiling, friendly from a distance, the sound of birds singing, welcoming the presence of travelers, long vines, curves surrounded by trees, attractive, eye-catching, eager to indulge in the beauty of culture, reveal the meaning of heritage associated with natural beauty.
The Sambor Prei Kuk temple site is built on a 3,000-hectare flat land located in Sambo village, Sambo commune, Sambo district, Kampong Thom province. Located along Stung Sen, this historical cultural site is about 28 km northeast of Kampong Thom provincial town.
During a visit to Preah Vihear and Kampong Thom provinces last weekend of June 2020, a delegation from the Ministry of Environment and a group of journalists also visited cultural and historical sites in the central part of the country. Enjoy the beautiful scenery with a lot of photo activities as a souvenir, post drunk on social media such as Facebook, Telegram and so on.
Passing from one temple to another, our pedestrian delegates encountered many strange sights in this resort, or in other words, being in a secret camp hidden in a majestic divine capital, as if no one had seen it. Before coming. Ask how exciting such a feeling is?
Please be informed that the Sambor Prei Kuk temple site was inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List at 9:40 pm (Cambodian time) on July 08, 2017 at the 41st Summit of the World Heritage Committee. Held on July 2-12, 2017 in Krakow, Poland.
Most of the Sambor Prei Kuk temples were built by King Isanvarman I as a single temple and were built in groups within a single wall. The construction materials are brick, mortar, sandstone, while the temple layout is square and octagonal with carvings on mortar. The architectural ornaments are made of sandstone and the pillars are almost made of sandstone, while the stone staircase and door frame are also made of sandstone.
The group of temples in the Sambor Prei Kuk area is divided into three main groups and there are many smaller groups such as:
1. The northern group of temples or the Sambo group of temples, built in the early 7th century under the hand of King Isanvarman I, dedicated to Shiva, surrounded by many small groups of temples built of solid brick.
2. The Kandal Temple Group or the Lion Temple Group was built during the reign of Jayavarman II in the 9th century and is dedicated to Shiva. In addition to the Lion Temple in the center, there are seven other small temple towers built of brick in the early 7th century under the handiwork of King Isanvarman I.
3. The Southern Temple Group or the Yeay Poan Temple Group was built in the early 7th century under the handiwork of King Isanvarman I, with a central tower called Neak Poan Temple or Yeay Poan Temple, and seven secondary towers. Located in a two-story wall, the inner wall is made of solid brick dedicated to Shiva.
As for the other small group of temples, such as the Krol Romeas temple group, the Trapeang Ropak temple group, the Russey Rolek temple group, the Sandan temple group, and the Bos Ream temple group. Beautiful scenery this season, tourists should not miss the invitation to visit.
Investment companies request the Ministry of Agriculture to consider setting up a cashew crop research center!
(Phnom Penh): An investment company in cashew cultivation has requested the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries to consider increasing the cashew crop research center to conduct research related to diseases, production, soil quality, pests, fertilizers and drugs in order to help the crop production. Get sustainable results.
This proposal was made on the occasion of Mr. Veng Sakhon, Minister of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, on June 15, 2020, visiting and evaluating the progress of Sophorn Theary Commercial Co., Ltd., which is investing in economic land concessions. It is located in Chong Spean village, Khvav commune, Chi Kreng district, Siem Reap province.
Oknha Seak Piseth, director of the company, said that the company received permission from the Royal Government in 2006 to invest in more than 4,000 hectares of registered economic land concessions in Chong Spean village, Khvav commune. Chi Kreng district, Siem Reap province.
He said that the company has signed a contract to implement the project with the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries in 2017 on an area of 1,232 hectares for investment in cashew cultivation. Currently, 1,030 hectares have been planted, 48 kilometers of roads have been built, employing 46 people (permanent) and more than 1,000 part-time workers. On average, the yield was more than 500 kg / ha and last year yielded more than 250 tons.
He added that the challenges are: first, the lack of experts in cashew crops, and second: the crop has a lot of fungal diseases and pests, and third: susceptible to impact (pests and yields fall. Down) from climate change.
The director of the company proposed to the Minister that "the Cashew Crop Research Center should be established in the future to conduct research on diseases, production, soil quality, pests, fertilizers and drugs in order to help the production of this crop to achieve sustainable results. The company will look forward to collaborating on this research. ”
The Minister praised and appreciated the company that has worked hard and made all kinds of sacrifices to invest in cashew crops. For the future, the Minister suggested that the company should expand the cultivated area as planned and in particular should set up a cashew processing plant can get a higher value added than sell raw materials whose prices fluctuate according to the market.
The minister said that if the company can implement it, it will contribute to the development of this area to become a cashew processing industry in the future, to create jobs and absorb labor in this area as much as possible.
The Minister also urged the company to fully cooperate with the Forestry Administration, the Provincial Department of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries and local authorities to implement the law, guidelines, development plans of the Royal Government, the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries to use all land resources. Potentially effective both socially and economically.
