Disputes over the Temple of Preah Vihear erupted in 1954 after Cambodia gained independence from France. Thailand has sent a large number of armed forces to occupy the Temple of Preah Vihear.
Despite the Hague court's decision. But the Thai side respects only one point, the first point that determines that the temple of Preah Vihear is within the sovereignty of Cambodia.
In particular, the second point that the court ordered Thailand to withdraw forces from the temple and the area around the temple in Cambodian territory was not implemented by the Thai side. For Thailand, what the International Court of Justice has ruled is the temple of Preah Vihear. The 4.6-square-kilometer area around the temple belongs to Thailand and Thailand has the right to deploy its forces there.
Due to this position, in July 2008, Thailand raised troops to invade the Keo Sikkha Kirisvara pagoda next to the temple of Preah Vihear again. The attempted conquest of the Preah Vihear site came after the Preah Vihear temple was inscribed on the World Heritage List on July 7, 2008.
The border dispute between Cambodia and Thailand erupted again sharply, leading to an armed conflict. Many of the temples of Preah Vihear were hit by shrapnel from Thai soldiers. After the search for a way to end the conflict between the bilateral and ASEAN mechanisms failed, Cambodia appealed to the International Court of Justice in The Hague to reinterpret the 1962 ruling.
Finally, on 11 November 2013, the International Court of Justice (ICJ) reinterpreted the 1962 judgment and ruled that Cambodia had sovereignty over the entire territory of the Temple of Preah Vihear and ordered the Thai side to withdraw its armed forces and guards. All the temples leave the perimeter of the temple.
Ministry of Agriculture urges investors to set up paper processing and processing plant in Cambodia rather than transporting raw materials to Thailand
