Understand the law: Food, legal obligations
(Phnom Penh): In general, the living conditions of individuals in society are not the same, such as the economic and financial situation, in which we see that some individuals have some wealth, some are poor and some fall. Becoming a poor person and a beggar, for example.
Depending on this, with kindness and generosity between each other, each person must know how to share and help each other, as the Khmer proverb says to know how to help each other in times of need. In addition, the existing culture in the society that requires parents to have cases of child rearing, children, adoptive parents, siblings, support each other is a case of moral duty that has been adhered to by the Cambodian people from the beginning.
At the same time, there are some situations where children are ungrateful to their parents, parents abandon their children, brothers and sisters do not know how to help each other in difficult times, family members are divided, they do not support each other, which is a situation that affects the morality, good tradition of the society. Well.
Therefore, in order to better understand Fresh News, in cooperation with the Cambodian Lawyers for International Cooperation, please comment on the topic of "Legal Obligation of Food" to share with the people and students as follows:
According to the Civil Code of the Kingdom of Cambodia, mandatory food is a financial aid to individuals who are unable to support themselves on their own. A person who needs compulsory food is called a person who is entitled to a mandatory meal or a person who provides a mandatory meal is called a person who has a mandatory meal case.
Compulsory meals are generally categorized as compulsory meals provided by the state, compulsory food arising from court decisions in the event of divorce (joint obligatory meals and obligatory meals for minors) and private obligations as required by law.
According to the Civil Code, people who are obliged to supply compulsory food are defined among certain relatives with those who are entitled to compulsory food. Obligatory meals defined by this law are private obligatory meals that provide special benefits in the relationship of a private individual and exclude the relationship between spouses (spouses) and the relationship between parents and minors.
Compulsory food duties are counted from the first grade onwards, if there are no relatives in the first grade, the relatives shall be determined in the following order as described above. In addition to the persons designated above, the court may order a relative or kinsman (in the case of the death of a spouse) in the third tier to be responsible for the case of obligation food supply, if there are special circumstances at the request of the person entitled to the obligation meal.
The level and mode of supply is decided by the deliberations of the persons in charge of the supply or by the court's decision, taking into account the necessities of life of those persons, the affordability of the persons in charge. And all other situations.
The right to demand compulsory food is prohibited by law from being imposed, as this right is the exclusive right of a person in need of obligatory food that cannot be used as a subject of concessions, grants or the creation of a deed. Possession for third parties or other businesses.
In summary, we see that obligatory food is a type of civil right and obligation that defines a kinship relationship, focusing on the economic and financial provision of one or more specific relatives or persons with related needs. And their daily lives in accordance with the law.
