Cambodia has nearly 9,000 hectares of Pailin Mien plantation, Minister Veng Sakhon discusses with Mien Pailin community to find solutions to market problems
(Pailin): Mr. Veng Sakhon, Minister of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, on June 14, 2020, came to meet and discuss with the Mien Pailin community to find solutions to the market problems that the community has been facing.
The meeting took place in O'Rel Village, Stung Karch Commune, Sala Krao District, Pailin Province, with the participation of the Deputy Governor, the Director of the Provincial Department, the Armed Forces, local authorities and about 150 community members.
Mr. Phan Chanthul, Provincial Governor, read the report to the Minister and guests briefed on the geographical situation, administration, security, safety, cooperation and agricultural work, especially the various agricultural products that Pailin Province can produce, including red corn, cassava, soybeans. Mean Pailin, Mean Kraom, Mango, Orange, Pursat, Rambutan, Duren, Knor, Teap Trabek and Rubber on a total area of 69,246 hectares.
In order for the community members to better understand the technical work, the Minister also instructed Mr. Ngin Chhay, the Royal Government of Cambodia delegate in charge of the Director General of the General Department of Agriculture and technical officers to continue to explain and explain the technical work related to the requirements for export. General and separate agricultural products of this Pailin longan product.
According to the head of Pailin Mean community, currently Pailin has a total cultivated area of more than 3,250 hectares, yielding more than 32,500 tons per year, which produces jobs for the people. At the base were thousands. In addition to selling fresh lentils, the community also organizes small and medium-sized processing production chains.
Currently, the community has been facing important issues that the community has asked the Minister and relevant institutions to intervene in a timely and effective manner, including:
First, the difficulties in exporting Cambodian Pailin longan products to foreign markets. Second: The procedure for registering longan plantations in accordance with the principles of good agricultural practices. Third: The procedure for applying for ISO quality certification.
According to the report of the General Department of Agriculture, in 2019, the area under Pailin longan cultivation was 8,925 hectares and the yield was 89,257 hectares.
