Indian Embassy in Cambodia: This year's International Yoga Day is celebrated under the theme "Yoga at Home Yoga with Family"
(Phnom Penh): As part of the celebration of the 6th International Yoga Day this year, the Indian Embassy in Cambodia is planning to hold Yoga Week from 15-20 June 2020 in Phnom Penh and 23 June 2020 in Siem Reap.
In an interview with the media, Manika Jain, Indian Ambassador to Cambodia, said that International Yoga Day is celebrated annually on June 21 to raise awareness of the benefits of physical health and fitness. Psychology derived from ancient Indian yoga practice.
Yoga is considered to be a traditional practice that originated in India about 5,000 years ago. This classic form of health exercise is a spiritual discipline that focuses on bringing harmony between body and mind. It also includes various techniques called asana (martial arts) and training the mind through breathing and meditation techniques.
Yoga seems to be a technology for the well-being of the human body. And it is important at a time when there is a global epidemic because yoga is recognized as an important tool in maintaining good health, boosting the immune system and reducing stress.
Five International Yoga Days have been successfully celebrated in Cambodia by the Embassy of India in collaboration with the Ministry of Tourism, the Ministry of Culture and Fine Arts and the Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports. These events have been overwhelmingly attended throughout these years and have become very popular due to their importance in physical and mental health. The Royal Government of Cambodia has been supporting the raising awareness of yoga practice.
(Phnom Penh): The location of the Chinese wine handicraft venue "Tang Kui", which caused poisoning and death of 7 people in Malai district and Poipet city, Banteay Meanchey province, a few days ago, was cracked down on by the joint authorities. And came to check the handicraft location in Bak Kheng Leu village, Sangkat Bak Kheng, Khan Chroy Changva in Phnom Penh.
The operation to inspect the production and distribution of Chinese medicine took place on the morning of June 13, 2020 at a house in Bak Kheng Leu village, Bak Kheng commune, Chroy Changva district, Phnom Penh.
The operation was led by the leadership of the General Department of Consumer Affairs and Mr. Heng Maly, Branch Manager of the General Department of Consumer Protection, Competition and Fraud Suppression and was coordinated by Mr. Say Nora, Deputy Prosecutor of the Phnom Penh Municipal Court. With the cooperation of the Economic Crimes Police of the Phnom Penh Municipal Commission, the Criminal Police of the Ministry of Interior, and with the participation of local authorities.
During the search operation, the police found some materials left after the owner destroyed and closed the door.
It should be noted that on the morning of June 11, 2020, the joint police cordoned off the location and searched the above location because the owner woke up before locking the door to escape, so the police postponed the search this morning.
According to Deputy Prosecutor Say Nora, although the owners were aware of and destroyed the exhibits, they were not exhausted and the authorities found more evidence left behind.
Mr. Say Nora added that to date, seven people have died and more than 40 others are in the emergency room due to alcohol poisoning.
