The following is the message of Samdech Techo Hun Sen: Mr. Neth Pheaktra: Loss of habitat, encroachment on natural grassland as a reason for the decrease in the number of whispers
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State and Spokesman for the Ministry of Environment, pointed out that the reason for the decrease in the number of whispers is due to the loss of habitat, encroachment on natural grassland land, conversion of natural grassland land into rice fields. Dry hunting by local people, the use of poisons and the effects of other factors.
Mr. Net Pheaktra made this remark during a visit to inspect the implementation of the Whistle Conservation Project in collaboration with the Ministry of Environment and the Wildlife Conservation Society (WCS) in Stong District, Kampong Thom Province on 10 June. 2020.
Mr. Net Pheaktra said that whispers are rare and belong to the most endangered bird species in the world, which requires the protection and conservation of all stakeholders, especially the people. In areas where whispers live, they lay their eggs.
At the same time, Mr. Neth Pheaktra said that the remarkable number of whispers studied and recorded is stable in protected areas with active law enforcement, and it has dropped sharply outside protected areas. And in areas where law enforcement activities and community participation in protected areas are still limited.
In front of the community in Stong district, Kampong Thom province, Mr. Neth Pheaktra thanked the people who cooperated in the conservation of whispers and successfully implemented the sustainable rice project. He urged the people to continue to work with the Royal Government of Cambodia to protect the world's endangered species for the next generation.
The senior official added that through the Whisper Conservation Project in the Northern Tonle Sap Landscape Protection Area in Kampong Thom and Siem Reap Provinces, the local communities have been improving their livelihoods through the implementation of the Rice Sustainability Project. Organic rice production, export and income from eco-tourism.
(Phnom Penh): On the morning of June 13, 2020, a group of police officers executed the order of Mr. Keo Sothea, Prosecutor of the Phnom Penh Municipal Prosecutor's Office, to detain a woman who was a suspect in the case of threatening, insulting and inciting Discrimination. However, the operation failed because the suspects and accomplices shook hands and did not allow the authorities to arrest them.
This strange case happened at Bat Doeng market, Ksem Ksan commune, Oudong district, Kampong Speu province, where the suspect was Yim Eng, a 38-year-old female, residing in Village 1, Sangkat Boeung Salang, Khan Toul Kork, Phnom Penh. The suspect was charged by Mr. Keo Sothea with threatening, insulting and inciting discrimination, and a warrant was issued on June 12, 2020, ordering him to appear before July 2, 2020.
According to the complaint, the victim demanded a public apology and legal services, costs, loss of time, compensation for mental illness, damage to honor, reputation, dignity and other damages of one million US dollars.
