(Stung Treng): The Stung Treng Provincial Gendarmerie has launched an operation to crack down on looting of people's houses and taking more than 30,000 dollars in property after close cooperation from the relevant authorities. On June 11, 2020, the police sent the perpetrators to court to be punished according to the law.
The operation was carried out under the direct command of General Sao Sokha, Deputy Commander and Commander of the National Gendarmerie, and led by Brigadier General Ieng Vandy, Commander of the Stung Treng Provincial Gendarmerie in collaboration with the Kampong Speu Provincial Gendarmerie, Preah Vihear, Banteay Meanchey, Oddar Meanchey and Siem Reap.
According to the report, the suspect who was cracked down on by the police, 36-year-old Pech Phanna, committed aggravated theft (burglary) and took more than $ 36,000 in assets on June 6, 2020. At Thmalat village, Srah Russey commune, Stung Treng city.
After receiving complaints from the people and good cooperation from relevant institutions until June 8, the suspect was cracked down by the military in Kralanh district, Siem Reap province.
However, until June 11, 2020, the Stung Treng Provincial Gendarmerie sent the suspect to the Provincial Court to be punished according to the law, while the property of the victim, with the permission of Mr. Chet Khemara, was examined by the Provincial Gendarmerie Office. Stung Treng also returned to the victims. Spokesman for the General Commission: On June 11, another 1,314 vehicles were inspected and fined
According to the spokesperson of the General Commissioner, within 42 days of tightening the road traffic law by increasing the fine according to Sub-Decree No. 39, from May 1 to June 11, 2020, the traffic police nationwide inspected And fined a total of 59,686 vehicles, including 43,969 motorcycles and 15,717 vehicles of all types.
Notable offenses for motorcycles are mostly helmet, offenders, overweight and cell phone use. In particular, the offenses for cars are mostly speeding, no driver's license, no seat belt, violation of overweight signs, use of phones without license plates and so on.
Lt. Gen. Chhay Kim Khoeun continued that for 42 days, the implementation of the new sub-decree to fine the offending vehicle went well.
