Mr. Neth Pheaktra: Reasons for the decline of Cambodian vultures due to drug pollution, loss of habitat and lack of food
(Preah Vihear): Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Environment, stated that the reasons why vultures in Cambodia are declining due to the greatest threat from human pollution, habitat loss, food shortages, poaching Traps.
This confirmation by Mr. Neth Pheaktra was made after the opening of the annual vulture census in Cambodia, which took place in the community of Dang Phlit - Vulture Restaurant in the geographical area of Chhep Wildlife Sanctuary in Chhep District, Preah Vihear Province. Participated by provincial authorities, Prey Lang Baitong project officials and professional officials of the Ministry of Environment on June 10, 2020.
Mr. Pheaktra said that communities in some areas have used poisons in or around ponds to catch birds and mammals, and that pollution is also a major cause of vulture deaths. They ate the corpses of dead animals due to poison.
He said that vultures are a rare species and are at high risk of extinction globally, while the number of vultures continues to decline every year and to a worrying extent, despite the Royal Government through the Ministry of Environment. With partner organizations working hard to protect and preserve.
Because vultures are endangered in the world and Cambodia is known to have the largest number of vultures left, the Secretary of State for the Ministry of Environment said that the Ministry of Environment has partnered with the Wildlife Rescue Association (WCS). Implemented conservation activities in protected areas through supplementary feeding and protection of nesting nests, increasing in-depth understanding of vulture risk factors.
Mr. Net Pheaktra confirmed that the vulture census was conducted at five different locations, including: in Siem Pang in the Siem Pang Wildlife Sanctuary in Stung Treng Province, at the Eagle Restaurant at Boeung Toal Field in Prey Andong Dang Plit community in the sanctuary. Wildlife in Preah Vihear province, in Sre Pok wildlife sanctuary, Mondulkiri province, in Angkor rice in Sesan area, Stung Treng and Lumphat wildlife sanctuary in Ratanakkiri province.
According to the December 2019 census, there were 44 vultures at the same site. The Secretary of State for the Ministry of Environment said that the current census of vultures is very important to know the true number of vultures in Cambodia and the development situation around the number of vultures in Cambodia.
According to Mr. Net Pheaktra, the results of the vulture census in 2019 show that Cambodia has 137 vultures of the three species, and the area with the most vultures is in the Siem Pang Wildlife Sanctuary in Stung Treng Province. And Chep Wildlife Sanctuary in Preah Vihear Province.
