Bank for Rural Development and Agriculture issues statement rejecting claims that the bank demands real estate appraisal fees from customers
(Phnom Penh): On the evening of June 10, 2020, the Bank for Rural Development and Agriculture issued a press release dismissing some allegations that the bank has demanded real estate appraisal fees to secure debts, stamp fees, lawyers or notaries on food loan contracts. And the cost of registering, establishing a mortgage or mortgaging real estate, securing debts from bank customers.
A press release obtained by Fresh News this evening states that the Bank for Rural Development and Agriculture would like to inform the public, farmers, business owners and customers that each bank always has a policy. And the respective credit procedures, and these principles and procedures are not much different. Especially related to the above expenses.
For the policy and procedure of credit of the Rural Development Bank and Agriculture, in addition to the obligation to pay interest and principal, there is only the cost of the procedure (the cost of administrative services of the bank), which is the burden of the customer to pay to the bank. This cost is stated in the loan agreement between the customer and the bank. For other expenses, please specify as follows:
1: Property appraisal for collateral: It is the customer's direct responsibility when applying for a mortgage, and this cost is paid to an independent licensed real estate appraisal company.
2. Cost of stamping attorney or notary on food loan contract: is the direct responsibility of the client and this cost is deducted from the customer's credit amount to be handed over to the attorney or notary.
3. The cost of registration, establishment of mortgage or mortgage on the mortgage with the relevant institutions is the responsibility of the client.
At the same time, the Rural Development Bank and Agriculture encourages the public, farmers, business owners and customers to contact the bank directly in case of any questions or inquiries about credit information or related fees to avoid any possible confusion.
At the same time, Mr. Kao Thach, General Director of the Rural Development Bank and Agriculture, considered that those mentioned were from those who had never borrowed money from the bank or who knew the story but did not know and had bad intentions for the development of the bank. If you have been involved in banking services, such as working in a bank, you know this.
