Vietnam ready to take the lead in wind power generation along the long coast
Vietnam is seen as trying to become a leader in generating electricity through wind turbines along its more than 3,400 kilometers of coastline. This was released by CNA on Wednesday morning, June 10, 2020.
Vietnam as a whole now has a total generating capacity of 54 gigawatts (1 GW = 1,000 megawatts), and the Vietnamese government is looking to double its output capacity to 130 gigawatts over the period. The next 10 years. Therefore, the construction of a wind turbine network is a proof that the above plan may achieve the desired results.
Around the establishment of wind power zones along the coast of Vietnam, the Danish energy company DEA has calculated that it will produce up to 160 gigawatts of electricity if it is fully operational. This calculation was made by the DEA with the Vietnam Renewable Energy Authority (REAV).
Vietnam has favorable plans to build a wind power network because it has a coastline of more than 3,400 km. DEA experts say that if the plan goes smoothly, in the next few years, Vietnam will be the leader in Southeast Asia in wind power generation.
(Phnom Penh-Stung Treng): A group of professional thieves who stole money from Wing and many other places that used to operate in almost all capitals and provinces were finally cracked down on by three of the five gang members to Sesan district. Stung Treng Province.
The operation was carried out by the Bureau of Serious Crimes of the Criminal Police Department in collaboration with the Phnom Penh, Stung Treng and Mondulkiri Provincial Police under the direct command of General Neth Savoeun, National Police Commissioner, Lt. Gen. In Bora, Commissioner General. Deputy Planner and Lt. Gen. Neng Chuor, Director of the Criminal Police Department, with the coordination of the Prosecutor's Office.
Police confirmed that the group of suspects had committed the crime on June 3, 2020 at Veluwing House, Concrete Road, Prasat Village, Sangkat Sno, Khan Kambol, caused by 5 suspects to go shopping at Valu Wing. When the victim was busy selling, the suspect entered the cash register and stole $ 10,000.
Currently, the suspect was brought to the Criminal Police Department for questioning and case processing, and then sent back to Stung Treng province for legal action.
Two children were killed and 22 others were seriously injured when an Oukong bus plunged into a stream in Oddar Meanchey province's Anlong Veng district.
(Oddar Meanchey): At least two boys died at the scene and 22 other men and women were seriously injured and transported to the hospital in a bus accident. On the morning of June 10, 2020, on the access road to the stream, on National Road 67 at the point of the bridge in Otameng village, Anlong Veng commune, Anlong Veng district, Oddar Meanchey province.
According to the traffic police of Anlong Veng District Police Inspectorate, the bus that crashed into the stream was owned by Ou Kong Company for night passengers. Prior to the incident, the car was carrying passengers from Phnom Penh to Anlong Veng district, a total of 24 people, including the car. Arriving at the scene at the bridge, the car swerved to the left, causing the entrance to the stream.
After the incident, Anlong Veng police arrived at the scene and rescued the seriously injured passengers and transported them to Anlong Veng District Referral Hospital for treatment. Authorities say two boys were killed and 22 others were seriously injured in the accident, which involved a pickup truck and a broken right thigh.
Regarding this case of traffic accident, the authorities concluded as a first step that it could be caused by a sleepy car in Taikung. However, the authorities have called on people to be careful when traveling, if not necessary, should choose a suitable time to travel instead of traveling at night.
More than 30 houses were hit by strong winds in Svay Chrum district
(Svay Rieng): At least 33 houses collapsed to the ground and their roofs were exposed after a heavy downpour mixed with strong winds but no danger to people and animals.
This terrible incident happened at 2:50 pm on June 9, 2020 in Kraol Kor commune, Svay Ang commune and Kork Pring commune, Svay Chrum district, Svay Rieng province.
Svay Chrum District Hall officials said that before the incident, there was a case of rain and strong winds, causing three communes in Svay Chrum district to collapse four houses and open 29 roofs.
Among the 33 damaged houses, the owner of the house, Pov Hoeun, a 57-year-old man living in Svay Ang village, Svay Angkor commune, said that his house had completely collapsed.
The source added that for the other 28 houses in Kraol Ko commune, Svay Chrum district, the roof was opened but did not cause any danger to life or injury.
Svay Chrum district authorities have called on residents to be extra careful when it rains, wind, lightning, to find a safe place, not to go out in the field, and to turn off their phones, televisions and radios, as these objects absorb current, making them more dangerous. Eventually.
Minister of Rural Development inaugurates construction of 13 rural roads with a length of more than 13 kilometers in Takeo province
(Takeo): Dr. Ouk Rabun, Minister of Rural Development, this morning, June 10, 2020, opened the construction site of a rural road under the framework of the project for cash for rural development and support farmers in Prey Chheuteal village, Taphem commune, Tram Kak district, Takeo province.
In Takeo province, there are 13 rural roads with a length of more than 13 kilometers to be built.
It should be noted that the Cash for Rural Development and Farmers Support Project is a project directly funded by the Royal Government in order to contribute to poverty reduction, improve the living standards and well-being of rural people and poor farmers.
This project is to be carried out through: 1. The creation of short-term job opportunities through the cash flow program, which will help solve the short-term emergency life and also create capital for further investment in agriculture or other suitable occupations. And 2- Development of rural roads and drainage infrastructure in urban areas.
The project covers 17 provinces, including Kampong Chhnang, Kampong Thom, Kampong Speu, Takeo, Battambang, Pursat, Prey Veng, Siem Reap, Svay Rieng, Oddar Meanchey, Kandal, Banteay Meanchey, Kampot, Kratie, Stung Treng, Tbong Khmum and Preah Vihear.
