Tbong Khmum province prepares 10 hectares of safe havens to evacuate people during floods during the rainy season
(Tbong Khmum): The National Committee for Disaster Management and Tbong Khmum Provincial Authority have set up a safe area on about 10 hectares of land to evacuate people during floods during the rainy season.
Residents in many districts in Tbong Khmum province are often flooded by the Mekong River during the rainy season. Safe places to evacuate people from the floods are a problem for the authorities in Tbong Khmum province.
Seeing the difficulties, the National Committee for Disaster Management and the provincial authorities have jointly set up a safe haven in Tonle Beit commune, Tbong Khmum district to evacuate people during the Mekong River flood. This safe haven covers an area of about 10 hectares and can accommodate about 3,000 families.
The National Committee for Disaster Management and provincial authorities are busy building the shelter. On June 9, 2020, Mr. Neang Chen, Advisor of the National Committee for Disaster Management and Mr. Chey Bunnara, Deputy Governor of Tbong Khmum Province, as well as relevant officials, held a meeting and inspected the construction process of this safe place.
Senior Minister Kun Kim, First Vice Chairman of the National Committee for Disaster Management, told Fresh News that people living along the Mekong River in districts in Tbong Khmum province are flooded every year by the Mekong River. . Temporary shelters during the floods are a problem and cause difficulties for the people.
The Senior Minister continued that after seeing this difficulty, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the Prime Minister of Cambodia and the Chairman of the National Committee for Disaster Management, arranged for the organization of this safe place.
Mr. Kun Kim confirmed that this safe place is made with the right shelter for people to stay temporarily during the flood.
Senior Minister Kun Kim thanked Deputy Prime Minister Chea Sophara, Minister of Land Management, Urban Planning and Construction and Chairman of the National Task Force for Tbong Khmum Province, for donating his own funds to build a shelter for evacuees. Vehicles during floods during the rainy season. Indonesia finds 1,043 new cases of COVID-19, bringing the total number of cases to 33,076
