The Minister stated that the reduction in the import of pigs into Cambodia follows the recommendation of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the Prime Minister, to increase the capacity of local breeding to reduce imports.
Speaking at a press conference after the signing ceremony of the exchange of fresh mango exports directly to China, Minister Veng Sakhon raised the implementation of the law by repealing the principle of importing pigs to companies that violate the law.
According to the Minister, Cambodia needs about 10,000 pigs per day, of which domestic production is up to 5,800 pigs per day, allowing the import of 6,000-7,000 pigs. Seven companies using surveillance measures if any companies commit crimes will reduce imports.
The Minister stated that the Ministry of Agriculture's implementation of crime suppression to avoid the problem of importing diseases, which leads to a significant impact on local breeding.
The minister added that the ministry was also considering further reducing pig imports, with no plans to increase imports to boost domestic breeding.
Senior Cambodian Police Officer Remembers the Great Merit of a Japanese Man Who Dedicated to Helping to Build Thousands of Schools in Cambodia
(Phnom Penh): After receiving the news of the death of Ta Sumita, a Japanese man who helped build thousands of schools in Cambodia, on June 9, 2020, General Mak Chito, Deputy Police Chief The nation, which has special memories with Grandpa Sumita, has dedicated his time and resources to helping Cambodia.
The following is the complete text of General Mak Chito:
Grandpa "Sumita" is always in the hearts of the Cambodian people!
An old Japanese man that I respect and love and take as a symbol after meeting the planet with him for a while. Through Mr. Kao Bunsong, I met Brother Sumita. The first thing I was interested in was a fresh smile, friendly words, gentle, polite, simple, polite gestures, sincere, honest, right, respectable, trustworthy.
Every time I meet him, of course, I never see him upset or complaining, even though he is old and has to travel through the rain, the wind, the long road, the rough road, no matter how difficult.
Sincerely, I had a close conversation, asked for experience from him, I still remember two issues that he described in detail:
1: I asked him about the experience that Japanese people strive to build and develop the country after World War II to become a modern developed country like today?
He described that when he first arrived in Cambodia, he arrived in Pursat province and met two orphaned boys with no food or shelter. Out of pity, he asked for help and sent the monks to live in the pagoda, but the chief priest told them that it was not possible because there were too many children in the pagoda and there was not enough food.
When he brought the boy back, he left a piece of bread and gave it to one of the children. He did not eat it alone. He tore it in two and gave it to another child.
I brought the request of parents, teachers and boys and girls who are in need of school buildings to Mr. Sumita. He paid attention and went to the village to the school. Achieving unforgettable achievements throughout Cambodia, such as the primary school building in Prey Veng province in Kandal province and the kindergarten building in Svay Rieng province.
On behalf of the government, on my own behalf, especially the parents, guardians, teachers and tens of thousands of Cambodian students, I would like to thank Skoled Japan, with Mr. Sumita as the representative!
I always imagine a kind, smiling face, most respectable! This Japanese man has attracted the hearts of Cambodians to love and respect as a model!
I would like to express my deep gratitude to the Royal Government of Japan, the people of Japan, for always supporting our Cambodian people in all circumstances with a sincere spirit of solidarity. Unforgettable memories.
