Thailand plans to re-attract international tourists after positive blocking of COVID-19
BANGKOK: The Thai government is launching plans to rebuild confidence and re-establish its country as a trusted tourist destination after travel restrictions to prevent COVID-19 were eased. This was released by CNA on Friday evening, June 5, 2020.
Thailand has decided to ban international flights until June 30, and international tourists are not expected to visit the Southeast Asian country immediately until the end of 2020. Currently, Thailand has a total of 3,102 cases of COVID-19, of which 58 have died and 2,971 have recovered. More than 40 factory workers in Svay Rieng special economic zone fainted for two days in a row
It should be noted that yesterday in the "Svay Rieng Gigarris" special economic zone in Doeum Po village, Kandeang Reay commune, Svay Teap, Svay Rieng province, 43 workers fainted in a row, according to some sources, due to a strong odor. The medicine stuck to the wire and the lack of oxygen caused the worker to have chest tightness and difficulty breathing, causing him to faint.
According to the Svay Teap district police, before the incident in the factory building, TY opened a warehouse to distribute the electric wire to the workers to wrap the light bulbs, and suddenly there was a bad smell spread all over the building and shortness of breath. Caused a series of suffocation of 43 workers who were working hard at the TY factory (light bulb manufacturer).
After a worker fainted, the police asked a friend to work with them to carry the victim out of the building and call an ambulance to transport 30 victims to Chi Phu Referral Hospital and 13 to Svay Teap District Referral Hospital. To rescue the other victims in the building who fainted, the factory owner was given a temporary rest and on June 6, 2020 to return to normal.
Doctors said that after examination and serum, some of the victims have recovered. Authorities concluded that there may have been a foul odor from the wiring and lack of oxygen in the large building without windows to allow natural air into the building, but in this case, officials of the Department of Labor and Vocational Training agreed. The inter-ministerial working group is working to find out the exact reason before providing it later.
