According to Mr. Puth Khean, chief of Tang Krasang commune, before receiving this pond, the local people used the pond water for living. Although the pond water is not clean due to the presence of animals, because his base has some parts that are difficult to dig wells due to lack of water source.
Not only in the dry season, people not only lack water to grow crops, even drinking water is difficult to find, because the pond water in the dry season almost evaporates. Therefore, the local people in Tang Krasang commune were very excited when the commune chief announced the digging of a new pond in the local area, which has a population of about 2,500 families.
In addition to daily use and for farming, the pond, which receives a grant from the Chinese government, also helps create jobs for local people. For example, Mr. Soy Toeur is a citizen who has been in the business of transporting water for sale to local people who need clean water and do not have time to pump water directly.
By getting clean water to use throughout the commune and create more jobs for the people. In particular, the rice fields of the people around the pond of Cambodia-China friendship can grow twice a year, whether dry or rainy. That is, there is enough water to use, which is why the people here are prosperous and prosperous again.
As a citizen who benefits from the pond, Ms. Leng Ran does not forget to thank the government and Chinese friends who helped make her commune beautiful.
The second phase of the water supply project is being implemented over a period of 24 months and is expected to cost a total of 12 million US dollars. The project is led by Mr. Ouk Rabun, Minister of Rural Development, and plans to build 964 pump wells, dig 12 common community ponds and 42 stone ponds.
The water supply project, funded by the Chinese government, is planned in 10 provinces, including Siem Reap, Banteay Meanchey, Battambang, Pursat, Kampong Chhnang, Kampot, Prey Veng, Svay Rieng and Kampong Cham. By the end of May 2020, the project is 31 percent complete.
It should also be noted that the Ministry of Rural Development, led by Ouk Rabun, has already received a second grant from the Chinese government worth about 83 million US dollars. The first step is to implement three projects. The first project is rural water supply, which started on May 30, 2017 and was completed on July 9, 2018. Which cost about 12 million US dollars.
The second project is the Rural Road Improvement Project, which applies to the construction of two-story paved roads (DBST) with a total length of 35,457 meters and a 125-meter concrete bridge in four target provinces. Including: Kampong Speu, Kampong Chhnang, Kampong Thom and Kampong Cham provinces. Launched on May 30, 2017 and completed in 2021 and costing a total of 11 million US dollars.
The second project is a 36-month project to improve rural roads, with a total cost of about 40 million US dollars. The project covers six provinces that are needed to transform rural areas. The six provinces are Kampong Speu, Kampong Chhnang, Kandal, Prey Veng, Svay Rieng and Tbong Khmum. The second phase of the Ministry of Rural Development's rural road improvement project is planned to be carried out on 18 roads with a total length of 119,467 meters.
This afternoon, Japan provided more than $ 41 million in grants to Cambodia for the implementation of three projects.
The Exchange of Letters and Minutes of the Discussion on the Granting of Scholarships was signed by Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon, Minister of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation, and Mr. Mikami Masahiro, Ambassador of Japan to Cambodia, held at the Ministry of Foreign Affairs. .
