(Phnom Penh): The Cambodian Microfinance Association, through its official Facebook page, has published an article highlighting the successful experience of a family in using a loan to start a business instead of migration.
The following is a full article by the Cambodian Microfinance Association on the use of loans by Mr. Phara and his wife, Phal Sophal:
His grocery business continued to grow, enabling him to make a fortune, including two plots of land, a house, a motorbike, and a large number of appliances, especially as he no longer migrated to work in Thailand. He now borrows money from two different microfinance institutions because he sees an opportunity to buy land next to his house to share with his children.
He shared his experience of borrowing money from two different institutions at the same time. First, the repayment gap between the two institutions must be far apart in order to be able to repay on time. Second, do not use loans for gambling or alcohol and spend sparingly to maintain a good reputation responsibly.
During Kovid 19, his daily income dropped from half a million riel to 250,000, however, he had no problem repaying, as he had savings, including savings. Also to repay the institution on time.
Mr. Phara and his wife stated that without the loans from the microfinance institution, he would not have this business and would not have the remaining land and house as he is today, and would not be close to his family, having to migrate to work in Thailand.
