Mr. Ngan Chamroeun, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Interior and Head of the NCDD Secretariat, said that the current structure of the health department has been transferred to the management structure of the capital-provincial administration, including 25 health departments. 25 capital-provincial referral hospitals, 103 OD offices, 92 municipal referral hospitals, 1,221 health centers and 127 health posts.
Mr. Mam Bunheng, Minister of Health, said on that occasion that although the role of management of the Department of Health has been transferred to the capital-provincial administration, the Ministry of Health still has some necessary roles. Such as creating laws, setting the agenda, national health policy, setting technical standards, providing guidelines, monitoring and evaluating the performance of the health system.
The Minister instructed the Capital and Provincial Administration to mobilize financial support, materials and techniques and use resources to provide health services within its jurisdiction effectively, transparently and accountably.
Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng said that the transfer of management functions to provide health services to the capital-provincial administration is a historic achievement of the decentralization and deconcentration reforms in Cambodia after more than 10 years of operation.
Samdech continued that the director of the Department of Health of the Capital and Provincial Administration shall be responsible to the council and the Board of Governors of the respective Capital and Provinces in managing, managing, providing technical support and monitoring and providing responsibility for the provision of health services.
In order to ensure that the Capital-Provincial Administration can start to manage the function of providing health services that have been transferred responsibly, Samdech advised the Capital-Provincial Administration to organize, disseminate and launch the Department of Health of the relevant Capital-Provincial Administration.
