Cambodian Entrepreneurs, Australian Government and Institute of Technology of Cambodia Collaborate to Promote Agri-Food Product Innovation
(Phnom Penh): The Royal Government of Cambodia has been working with the Australian Government and its partners to develop the agro-processing industry and to attract more domestic and international investment.
The development of this sector is important as some of Cambodia's export agricultural products are not processed locally. The collaboration will help create value-added opportunities as well as create more jobs in the agricultural sector, which accounts for 22 percent of GDP.
"The promotion of agricultural and food production is an important part of the Australian Government's contribution to the fight against the Kovid 19 crisis in Cambodia," said Pablo Kang, Ambassador of the Australian Embassy to Cambodia.
Mr. Pablo Kang added, "The Royal Government of Australia, through the CAVAC program, has been supporting the agro-food sector in Cambodia for many years. At this time, the Australian Government is pleased to partner with Cambodian entrepreneurs and the Cambodian Institute of Technology to build a foundation for the high-quality food industry in Cambodia, which will be produced by small and medium-sized Cambodian enterprises.
Dr. Chheang Vann Monin, Executive Director of Cambodian Entrepreneurs, said, “With the support of the Ministry of Economy and Finance, Cambodian entrepreneurs have a vision to become a national forum for promoting all activities of the entrepreneurial sector in Cambodia. This project is in one of the priority areas of Cambodian entrepreneurs is the processing of agricultural products.
"Apart from supporting the development of agro-food processing, small and medium enterprises can also receive other support from Cambodian entrepreneurs, such as capacity building and joint venture opportunities," he added.
