(Kampong Chhnang): On June 2, 2020, Major General Khov Ly, Provincial Police Commissioner of Kampong Chhnang Province, led a total of 120 police officers, with the participation of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces, the Royal Gendarmerie and many other village, commune and district authorities. Helping to prepare houses for people in Peam and Svay communes, Samaki Meanchey district, after suffering from strong winds that caused the collapse and opening of the roofs of about 40 houses.
Brigadier General Ear Bunthoeun, Deputy Commissioner in charge of Staff, said that rain, wind, gusts and lightning struck some areas in Samaki Meanchey district, affecting two communes on the evening and night of June 1, 2020, destroying 40 houses, but did not cause danger to human life.
On the morning of June 2, 2020, according to the preliminary report of the Samaki Meanchey District Authority, in Samaki Meanchey District, strong winds hit some villages in Peam and Svay communes, damaging more than a dozen houses. Forty of them, of which 35 roofs and walls were opened, five collapsed to the ground and three cows were killed by lightning.
On the occasion of leading the police force to help the people, Major General Khovly said that when the natural disaster of this fierce wind immediately occurred, all of our armed forces always cooperated with the working group of the People's Authority. Local people immediately responded by repairing the houses of the damaged people to provide immediate shelter for the affected people to shelter them.
Separately, the Royal Government through the Cambodian Red Cross, provincial authorities and local relief teams will also provide emergency food to them in the future to facilitate daily life to eat on time.
This situation has made the general public aware of the real faces of these human rights and democracy champions, as their masks are now open. In fact, they are not champions of human rights and democracy or freedom of the press, as they claim. Behind that group are purely political interests and agendas.
They work for the benefit of donors and foreigners behind their backs, just dropping money to survive. If something hurts the boss, they have to shut up. Conversely, if something benefits the donor ambitions, they open their mouths incessantly.
Based on what has been happening in the United States and in Europe now, as well as in the past, in Cambodia as well as in other countries, we can draw some conclusions as follows:
* First: Each country has its own problems, whether big or small, rich or poor. So, big countries, do not just look at the problems in the small countries and set yourself up as a mentor to those small countries, because one day the same problems will happen in their own country. If a problem arises, you will need to take the same steps to restore and maintain public order: social, economic and political stability.
* Second: It is not certain that large and developed countries can handle the situation better than small and less developed countries. Sometimes small countries can have better and more effective measures than big countries. The management of Kovid 19 and the management of social unrest in the United States are now examples. Sometimes a small country is more capable than a great country. So "stop being a big brother, be strong and look down on others."
* Third: When there are riots, there is always violence, which eventually leads to riots, beatings, arson and looting. This is why the authorities are wary of inciting or inciting demonstrations in a defensive way rather than a cure.
* Fourth: There is no champion in the world of human rights, democracy or freedom of the press. Each country has different customs, traditions and mindsets. Some countries value freedom, while others value discipline and order. So respect each other's rights and help each other equally for the well-being of all people on the planet.
