(Phnom Penh): PRASAC has continued its efforts in the field of innovation and the provision of state-of-the-art financial technology services to customers by launching an ATM card service immediately in order to streamline the card issuance process. Easier, simpler and faster.
Say Sony, PRASAC's Executive Vice President for Marketing, said that customers can now get their ATM card immediately when opening a PRASAC Savings Account without having to wait for several days and come to the branch again to receive it. Get an ATM card. In addition, customers can also receive a new card to replace a lost or damaged card immediately.
Mr. Sony added that this is a new service in addition to the rich and modern financial products and services that PRASAC offers. He also expressed his pleasure that the launch of this new service will add value to PRASAC customers by eliminating the waiting time.
He said that as a large financial institution in Cambodia with a unwavering commitment to customer service by giving priority to customers and a high commitment to provide the best customer service experience. In this sense, the immediate delivery of ATM cards will provide customers with a faster and better service experience.
He stated that with PRASAC ATM card, customers can manage cash by themselves, such as transferring money to business partners or loved ones, transfer money by password, pay for water, electricity, buy phone cards, check account balances and other transactions at PRASAC ATMs at Nationwide, all the time. Open a PRASAC Savings Account today to get a beautiful ATM card immediately with high interest rate from PRASAC.
Siem Reap Provincial Administration Shows Achievements and Directions in the Press Conference of the Royal Government Spokesperson's Office (Video inside) (Siem Reap): Siem Reap Provincial Administration presents its achievements in the past as well as directions for Implemented in the future through a press conference organized by the Spokesperson of the Royal Government on the morning of June 2, 2020.
The press conference was chaired and coordinated by Mr. Phay Siphan, Minister Delegate to the Prime Minister and Chairman of the Royal Government Spokesperson Unit, while the delegation of the Siem Reap Provincial Administration was led by Mr. Ly Samrith, Deputy Governor and attended by the Director, Deputy Director As well as other relevant officials.
Achievements presented by Mr. Ly Samrith, Deputy Governor of Siem Reap include:
The province plans to open two new OWSOs this June: Prasat Bakong and Banteay Srei, in addition to the five existing districts. For districts that have not yet established OWSOs, including Angkor Thom, Srey Snom, Angkor Chum, Varin, Svay Leu districts, will be operated through the OWSO mechanism, and the provincial administration has completed the construction project. Out in 2020.
* Infrastructure work: The provincial administration has used the funds for the construction and repair of 27 provincial roads with a total length of 16,450 km and installed drains and box drains along the roads. In addition, the provincial budget was used to repair 7,284.2 square meters of damaged roads in the city and drew 4,058 square meters of traffic signs.
For the national budget, through the Ministry of Public Works and Transport, 4 lines (6.92 km) were built (anniversary maintenance), including 6.1 km of DBST road, 0.82 km of concrete road, and 4 drains were installed along the road. 88 km of drainage through 12 roads and maintained 19 national roads, 412.84 km of paved provincial roads and 20 red gravel provincial roads with a length of 271.41 km.
The Ministry of Rural Development's budget is used to maintain 287.5 km of anniversary roads, including 159.8 km of red gravel roads, 2 km of rubber roads and 125.7 km of DBST roads.
Irrigation: The province has a total of 158 small, large and medium irrigation systems that can irrigate 148,172 hectares. The province also paid attention to the repair and maintenance of sluice gates, canals and completed the construction of irrigation dams of Makak, Spean Sreng and Ksak dams, which totaled 5,175 meters of dams and dug 60,108 meters of canals. Kep) Create two water-using communities.
* Clean water: The province has expanded its capacity to produce 30,000 cubic meters of clean water per day, equivalent to 50% of the population needs in Siem Reap town, which is up to about 60,000 cubic meters in One day, there will be a total of 11,028 customers using clean water in the city.
Separately, for the project to build a factory with a capacity of 60,000 cubic meters per day, as well as the main network and distribution network of 166 kilometers, and is implementing a construction contract, a loan from the Japan International Cooperation Agency (JICA).
* Electricity work: Power supply work in the province in 2019 was distributed throughout 100 communes / sangkats of the province, which can supply a total of 159,344 customers, an increase of 13,596 customers, equivalent to 866 villages, an increase of 55 villages. Of the 927 villages in the province (910 officially recognized villages), the remaining 51 villages do not have access to electricity.
The province has two power substations, the first substation in Puok district has a supply capacity of 75 MW and the second substation in Prasat Bakong district has a supply capacity of 150 MW, both of which meet the maximum demand of the province up to 119.10 MW. .
Indeed, the work direction for 2020, the provincial administration has made a comprehensive vision to determine the strategy in each phase and in line with the actual situation of the evolving socio-economic situation to meet the needs of the people and maintain Stability, security, public order, natural environment, culture, morality, society, dignity and poverty reduction of the people.
Banteay Meanchey Provincial Administration Receives 4 Tons of Rice and Some Foodstuffs from Donors
(Banteay Meanchey): Banteay Meanchey Provincial Administration received donations including 4 tons of rice, 200 sticks of soy sauce, 200 sticks of fish sauce from Mr. Chea Phong Leang, Mrs. and Mrs. and their children to contribute to the Royal Government of Cambodia and the Banteay Meanchey Provincial Authority. Fight against Kovid 19 and share solutions to people in need.
The grant agreement was signed on June 1, 2020, and this is another legal document after the exchange of letters signed on March 24, 2020 between the Ministry of Foreign Affairs and the Ministry of Foreign Affairs. Cambodia International Operations and the Embassy of Japan in Cambodia, which may lead to the implementation of subsequent procedures for the implementation of the actual project.
A joint statement between the Ministry of Economy and JICA, obtained by Fresh News on June 2, 2020, stated that the project aims to improve access to safe drinking water in Takhmao City. Duration:
1: Construction of a water treatment plant with a capacity of 30,000 cubic meters per day 2: Construction of raw water pumping station and raw water pipeline 3: Construction of water distribution equipment 4: Installation of SCADA system To monitor and control the operation of the factory as well as the transfer of technology for the operation and maintenance of the factory using experience and knowledge from Japan.
Currently, the clean water used in Takhmao City is mainly supplied from Phnom Penh. Therefore, this project will contribute significantly to improving not only the water supply service in Takhmao and surrounding areas, but also to reduce the water supply from Phnom Penh.
Construction of the project will take 29 months and is scheduled for completion in 2023. This is the 12th project from 1993 to the present that the Royal Government of Japan, through JICA, has provided to the Royal Government of Cambodia in the field of water supply, and is also the sixth grant. To the Phnom Penh Water Supply Authority.
This project will make a significant contribution to assisting the Royal Government of Cambodia in achieving the National Strategic Development Plan on 100% Urban Water Supply by 2025 by supporting the Phnom Penh Water Supply Authority, which is clearly responding. To the priority needs of Cambodia to expand the water supply system in line with the first angle of the fourth rectangle, as stated in the fourth rectangular strategy document of the Royal Government of Cambodia.
