(Kampot): About 18 families have recently filed a complaint to the Governor of Kampot Province to intervene to determine their boundaries in Toeng Tngay village, Boeung Touk commune, Teuk Chhou district, Kampot province recently. The villagers allege that a handful of brokers were buying land illegally and encroaching on their land.
Seeing this, Mr. Chiao Tai, Governor of Kampot Province, assigned Mr. Pil Kosal, Deputy Governor in charge of Land Management at the provincial level, on the afternoon of May 29, 2020, to lead a team from relevant institutions, departments and authorities to measure and demarcate the land for the people. Living in Boeung Touk commune, Teuk Chhou district and Andong Khmer commune, Kampot city, Kampot province, these 18 families avoid any abuse from other criminals.
In front of the 18 families in the disputed area, Mr. Pil Kosal said that he came here for the purpose of inspection, requiring experts to measure and set up specific boundary markers for the 18 families. Filed a complaint to the Kampot Provincial Administration recently.
He added that the visit to measure the boundary or determine the foot of the meter by throwing stone border posts to the people because the 18 families have a certificate has already measured the data from the students volunteering to measure the land of Samdech Techo Hun Sen In the tiger skin campaign during late 2012 and early 2012.
Land The land of the above people is in the forest cover, which is currently managed by the Ministry of Agriculture and does not have a title deed yet.
Mr. Pil Kosal also confirmed that the visit was not measured to make a title deed or distribute land to any citizen. Just measure and define specific boundaries for the people who have the title deed and the people who have their own clear land, which already fills the data of Samdech Techo's surveyed student generation.
Separately, for the parties to the dispute who bought the land in the past, the Deputy Governor also requested all relevant parties to present the relevant documents to easily resolve and if the measurement is not. There is no overlap, it is possible to set up border posts without any further protests.
According to the residents of the 18 families, they complained to the provincial authorities, while Mr. Bun Sophat and Mr. Tan Meng bought land from others but encroached on their land with the correct title deed.
However, the dispute arose when Mr. Bun Sophat, a resident of Sovansakor village, Kampong Kandal commune, Kampot city, and Mr. Tan Meng, a resident of Phnom Penh, bought land from another person located next to each other and encroached on the land. Theirs.
