(Battambang): Mr. Lim Kean Hor, Minister of Water Resources and Meteorology, and his colleagues this morning, May 30, 2020, continued their mission to inspect the water condition at the beginning of this rainy season to the construction site of Stung Sangke Dam and Medoun Teav Canal of Sala Ta On Dam Development Project is located in Ek Phnom and Sangke Districts, Battambang Province.
Accompanying the Minister to inspect the above location, the Director of the Department of Water Resources and Meteorology of Battambang Province and the technical working group informed the Minister about the current progress of the construction of the Ta On Dam project, which has been funded by the Republic of Korea. So far, about 65 percent.
Please note that the purpose of the project is 3 points:
1: Water reservoir on Stung Sangke, which is considered as the third phase, the lower part of Stung Sangke, after the upper part, there is a multi-purpose reservoir, Sek Sak and Kanghat Dam before falling into Tonle Sap Lake. The project is capable of irrigating 10,400 hectares of wet paddy and 4,700 hectares of dry paddy.
2: A source of water to supply clean water to Battambang.
3: It is beautiful and attracts tourism potential to Battambang province.
This project is another new achievement of the Royal Government of Cambodia and the Republic of Korea under the wise leadership of Samdech Techo Prime Minister of Cambodia.
Minister Lim Kean Hor praised the construction companies for their hard work despite some obstacles and challenges and instructed the department to continue to monitor and cooperate with the local authorities to make the construction last longer. Long to serve the work of growing crops and water supply to the daily lives of local people.
World Bank approves $ 15 million aid to Cambodia for pre-employment medical training system
(Phnom Penh): The Executive Board of the World Bank on May 30 approved a US $ 15 million loan from the International Development Association (IDA) to support a project to strengthen the pre-entry medical training system. Serve to help Cambodia strengthen the pre-employment training of doctors in order to improve the quality of health care services to achieve better results.
According to the World Bank, in Cambodia, doctors usually work in secondary (intermediate) and tertiary (advanced) clinics, while primary care facilities are staffed by nurses and midwives. See medical care. The majority of general practitioners work in provincial hospitals (63%), while the vast majority of specialists are deployed in urban hospitals (79%).
To help Cambodia address these challenges, the project will assist the Department of Human Resource Development of the Ministry of Health in strengthening governance, medical professional education, including the strengthening of regulations and standardization for physician education, medical examinations, and completion. National study and technical assistance and exchange of knowledge and experience on specialist medical education.
The project will also support the development and implementation of capacity-based training programs by the University of Health Sciences and regional training centers in six specialized medical programs: General Medicine, Dentistry, Pharmacology, Nursing, Maternity, and laboratory.
These programs are prioritized in the recently developed National Strategic Plan for Pre-Employment Education in Cambodia for 2020-2025. All aspects of gender and social environment will be integrated into the development, design and implementation of training programs based on these capabilities in the future.
"Investing in capacity building for medical professionals by training them to acquire better knowledge and skills to provide services in emergencies such as: "The Kovid-19 outbreak and the strengthening of health sector governance are critical to Cambodia's health care system."
"The World Bank is committed to supporting Cambodia in its efforts to improve health care services for Cambodians, especially the poor and the most vulnerable."
