World Bank President vows to help Cambodia resolve Kovid-19 crisis and strengthen economic recovery
"The global crisis caused by the spread of Kovid-19 will have a major impact on the Cambodian economy by 2020," Ingona Dobracha said in a statement.
"The World Bank is committed to assisting Cambodia in dealing effectively with the Kovid-19 crisis and in strengthening its economic recovery and resilience in the future," she added.
The World Bank says the Kovid-19 outbreak is having a major impact on Cambodia's key economic growth drivers, including tourism, the export industry and the construction sector. More than 70 percent of economic growth and employment account for about 40 percent of the country's total employment.
In this situation, the poverty rate in 2020 could increase by 3 to 11 percent, which is higher than the baseline level, or in other words, when there is no Kovid-19, especially in Among the households involved in key sectors are tourism, construction, trade, manufacturing, and the garment industry, if they lose 50 percent of their income for six consecutive months.
At the same time, the fiscal deficit will be the highest in 22 years.
(Phnom Penh): The United States and China, the world's first and second largest economies, have become the most influential competitors in the world today. The rivalry over influence and the war of words between the United States has intensified since Donald Trump became president in 2017.
Problems that are seen as causing China and the United States to face each other and become more serious include trade competition, the South China Sea dispute, territorial disputes in Taiwan and Hong Kong. Recently, Chinese President Xi Jinping issued a message to strengthen the national defense force and announced that his army is ready to fight against the invasion of Chinese sovereignty.
On May 29, 2020, the Royal Academy of Cambodia held a roundtable discussion on "Geopolitical Confrontation between China and the United States." Many expert speakers on history and geopolitics shared views on the current Sino-US confrontation.
The speaker highlighted a number of challenges, including the possibility of a war between the two powers. Despite the current fierce rivalry between China and the United States, experts see no war of attrition.
Mr. Sok Touch, President of the Royal Academy of Cambodia, said in the roundtable discussion that the challenges of the great powers in the 21st century are much softer than those of the post-World War II powers, and their challenge is to focus on regional development. Economy.
Mr. Sok Touch stressed that "the two powers can not compete with the use of force in any case."
During the talks, Chinese and US historians and geographers, as well as Sok Touch, outlined the economic, arms, and diplomatic ties of the two countries.
In this regard, experts see that China is softer than the United States. In other words, whichever relationship is useful, China captures it all, there are not many conditions. But for the United States, it may be linked to conditions such as transparency, democracy, law and the economy. Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng told the new Governor of Kep Province and the Chairman of the Council to jointly lead officials to serve the people and promote the development of the province
(Kep): Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng, Deputy Prime Minister and Minister of Interior, this morning, May 29, 2020, told the new Governor of Kep Province, Mr. Som Piseth and Chairman of the Provincial Council to work together to lead officials to serve the interests of the people. Citizens will promote the development of the province, especially tourism, which is an important core sector.
Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng's urging was made on the occasion of the inauguration of the new Governor of Kep, Mr. Som Piseth, replacing Mr. Ken Satha, who was transferred to the Secretariat of the Office of the Council of Ministers, and Mr. Vov Sokha, Deputy Governor, replacing Mr. Som Piseth. . The ceremony was held at Kep Provincial Hall.
