General Meas Virith: During the 19-Kovid Crisis, Drug Crimes Increase Crime in the Greater Mekong Subregion
(Phnom Penh): The National Anti-Drug Authority of Cambodia (NACC) has joined an online meeting of the Coordinating Officers of the Memorandum of Understanding (MOU) on Drug Control in the Greater Mekong Subregion, which includes Cambodia, China, Myanmar, Laos and Thailand. Vietnam and the United Nations Office on Drugs and Crime (UNODC) on May 27, 2020 in Phnom Penh.
On that occasion, General Meas Virith, Secretary General of the National Authority for Combating Drugs, stated that due to the Covid-19 issue, we have not yet implemented the 11th Sub-Regional Drug Control Action Plan with the support of UNODC. And request our meeting to discuss and find an appropriate solution to implement this sub-regional action plan to respond to the current drug threat.
On that occasion, General Meas Virith said, "While we have the problem of Covid-19 disease, regional and global cooperation has declined, increasing the number of organized international criminal gangs. "Conspiracy with local drug offenders is becoming more and more active, which is becoming an obstacle to the implementation of the UNODC sub-regional plan."
At the same time, Cambodia has joined forces with the region and the world to work hard to prevent and take responsibility for the multi-sectoral strategy on drug control and to continue to actively implement the fight against illegal drugs throughout the country. The country cracked down on 9,806 drug crimes in 2019, detaining 20,490 suspects and seizing 750 kilograms of all kinds of drugs.
