(Phnom Penh): "Phnom Teak Traing" resort, a natural resort area in Rattanak Mondul district, Battambang province, has been established by the Royal Government of Cambodia as a natural heritage site.
In the sub-decree on the establishment of "Phnom Taek Traing" as a natural heritage site, obtained by Fresh News on May 27, 2020, it is stated that the establishment of "Phnom Taak Traing" as a natural heritage site aims to protect its ecological role. For the benefit of all generations, limit the use of biodiversity, natural resources, cultural resources and functions of the region.
The purpose of this establishment is to protect the natural beauty of the area and the natural values of the area for entertainment, sightseeing, education, scientific research and belief, all of which do not pose a threat to the local natural and cultural environment.
The sub-decree added that the establishment of "Phnom Tak Traing" as a natural heritage site also aims to provide natural and cultural services for sustainable use and promote the participation of local communities as well as the public in contributing to the management of protection. And conservation of biodiversity, natural resources and local cultural heritage.
The sub-decree also states that the "Phnom Taek Traing" natural heritage area covers an area of 79.82 hectares in Treng commune, Rattanak Mondul district, Battambang province.
The sub-decree designates the Ministry of Environment as the authority to organize and manage the "Phnom Teak Traing" natural heritage site in collaboration with the relevant ministries of Battambang Provincial Administration and the Armed Forces to ensure environmental protection, biodiversity conservation and Sustainable use of natural resources in accordance with the provisions of the law in force.
According to the Battambang Provincial Administration, there is also a long cave in Phnom Saek Traing that attracts tourists to visit and pray according to the beliefs of the people.
In addition to Phnom Teak Traing, on May 25, the Royal Government also decided to establish the "Seven Mountains" in Phnom Proek district, Battambang province, as a natural heritage site.
