Minister of Health inspects the work and health of passengers returning to Cambodia by night flight at Phnom Penh International Airport
(Phnom Penh): In addition to preventing the spread of the COVID-19 respiratory virus that is exploding and rapidly evolving around the world, the Minister of Health, Professor Mam Bun Heng, at midnight on On May 25, 2020, he visited the work and health of passengers from other countries to return to Cambodia by flight from the Republic of Korea.
Minister Mam Bunheng said, "In the past, we have observed that all 19 Kovid patients in Cambodia are cases of importation, so we will not loosen our grip and continue to tighten the work measures for our officials at all levels. "High attention to the prevention of Kovid 19."
In fact, tonight there were two flights from the Republic of Korea with a total of 100 passengers, Cambodians, Koreans, French, Americans, Iraqis, in which our officials do not allow all passengers to return home, to take a car Transport them to health checkpoints located at the Royal Cambodian Air Force Air Force and Chak Angre Krom Health Centers to be tested for 19 Kovid overnight.
"Not only for flights from Korea, but all passengers returning to Cambodia must undergo a 14-day quarantine and 19 Kovid test to avoid the high risk of spreading Kovid 19," he said. Come to Cambodia. "Even if the results are negative, the passenger is required to continue to perform the test at home for another 14 days."
On that occasion, Minister Mam Bun Heng continued to call on the people to continue to protect and be vigilant in accordance with the protective measures of the Ministry of Health to be consistent under any circumstances.
He said the raccoon was found in the O Thmor area in the Sangkha Rukhavorn community forest within the Sangkha Ruk Voan wildlife sanctuary and received first aid before the raccoon died. . The carcass was buried by park officials and the Prey Sang Ruk Vorn community, led by Bun Salut, at his headquarters.
From the beginning of 2020 until May 24, 2020, four raccoons were found dead, one died in Kampong Speu province due to trapping and two others were found shot dead in Mondul province. Kiri and most recently, a raccoon died as a result of being trapped by hunters in the Sang Ruk Vorn Wildlife Sanctuary.
The Secretary of State for the Ministry of Environment says that the raccoon is an endangered species in the world and that Cambodia is proud that all people must participate in protecting and conserving wildlife by saying no to wildlife trafficking. Eat wild animals and do not hunt and catch wild animals.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra said that in 2019, rangers of the Ministry of Environment, together with the protected area community and partner organizations, removed about 20,000 traps from the 60 protected areas under the Ministry's conservation protection. Environment.
In late April 2020, a herd of seven raccoons was spotted in the Sre Pok Wildlife Sanctuary in Mondulkiri province.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra stated that the Ministry of Environment manages the protection and conservation of natural resources and wildlife present in the 30,254-hectare Sang Ruk Vorn Forest in Anlong Veng District, Chong Kal District and Samrong City, Oddar Meanchey Province. There is a community of Prey Sang Ruk Vorn led by Prince Bun Salout, Chief of Oddar Meanchey Province.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra said that the Sang Ruk Vorn Wildlife Sanctuary is rich in wildlife, especially some rare wildlife present in it, such as giraffes, rattlesnakes, monkeys.
He said that in the past, although members of the community forest, including Prince Bun Salout, community leader of Prey Sang Ruk Vorn, together with provincial authorities and park rangers to educate people about the participation in the protection of natural resources and wildlife conservation, but participation in Limited yet. Some crooks continue to steal, cut down trees, burn forests and hunt traps.
In the face of this problem, Secretary of State for the Ministry of Environment, Mr. Net Pheaktra, said that it is a threat to the work of protecting and conserving natural resources and affecting the sustainability of biodiversity in Cambodia.
