(Phnom Penh): Ms. O Vandin, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Health and spokesperson of the Ministry, revealed this morning, May 24, 2020, 19 new cases of Kovid have not been found, including the husband of a woman returning from The United States recently discovered 19 cases of Kovid on May 22.
In addition to updating the results of her sample of 19 cases of Kovid 19 from the Institut Pasteur, Ms. O'Vandin also answered questions from reporters on the health status, including measures to prevent the spread of the disease in women from the United States. Kovid 19 also.
The spokesperson described that from May 8, 2020, the day the above Cambodian women arrived in Cambodia, they were instructed to do cataracts at home for 14 days, with close monitoring by the group. Work. The Cambodian woman was infected by a passenger traveling from the United States to South Korea and was diagnosed with Kovid 19.
Ms. O Vandin said that after receiving information about the exposure of a Cambodian woman to a passenger entering South Korea with Kovid 19, the team also advised the 39-year-old Cambodian woman. He was tested on May 12, 2020 at the Khmer-Soviet Friendship Hospital, but had a negative result at the time.
Despite the negative results, the doctors advised the woman to continue to have cardiology at home for 14 days. Then, on May 21, 2020, the woman was tested again and got a positive Kovid-19 virus on May 22, 2020 and was sent to the hospital. Khmer-Soviet friendship doctor on the same night.
According to Ms. O Vandin, during the ritual at home, the woman had no one but her husband. The woman seems to be well aware of the risk of contracting Kovid 19. Before arriving home on May 8, 2020, she sent two children and a nephew to her hometown in Kampong Cham province to avoid To have a copy.
Ms. O Vandin added that since the woman's husband was a direct victim, she had already been tested with a negative result, but was required to undergo 14 more days of cataracts under the supervision of the team.
According to Ms. O Vandin, the flight from South Korea to Phnom Penh was carrying 19 women diagnosed with Kovid, carrying a total of 68 passengers (20 females and 48 males). Of these, 30 were Cambodians, 33 were Koreans, and some were Russians, Scythians and Austrians.
According to the team's research, apart from the Cambodian woman, no one on the plane with her had any health problems, and on the plane was arranged to sit apart. Individual safety gaps) as well.
The results also showed that apart from the woman's husband, who was at high risk for infection, 52 people were at low risk and 14 at medium risk.
In addition to providing information on the health status and history of exposure of patients from the United States, Ms. O Vandin continues to call on all Cambodians to continue to implement measures to prevent the transmission of Kovid 19. Always be careful not to be pacifist with this virus and remember that "it is easy to forget the Kovid 19 virus, but the Kavid 19 virus is not easy to forget us."
She went on to warn that the virus can be transmitted at any time, under any circumstances.
Please note that after more than a month of silence this week, Cambodia has found two more 19 Kovid cases. The other two cases were imported, one from the Philippines and one from the United States.
So far, Cambodia has found a total of 124 infected people, but 122 have been cured. Only two newborns remain under the intensive care of doctors.
