(Kampong Chhnang): Nowadays, old ox carts that have been remodeled for decoration in homes, resorts and parks are popular with customers who buy and display them in their homes. As a decoration to highlight the beauty of the home or in the park that looks admirable.
This wheat cart is not only for display as a home improvement or amusement park, it is also a maintenance and preservation of the ancestral heritage, because this type of ox cart is now almost extinct. And. This is according to a trader in Kampong Chhnang province who gave an interview to Fresh News Agency.
According to Mr. Suon Sineth, a trader who collects old ox carts to process and install new ones for sale, the recycling of old ox carts for sale in Svay Chrum commune is now becoming more and more popular throughout the country. With the people, government officials and rich people came to buy his ox cart in large numbers, which makes his business grow steadily.
The trader said that he now has a hard time collecting old cart parts to install new ones because he no longer wants to buy old carts in Kampong Chhnang province. Each ox cart he assembled sells for $ 400 to $ 1,200, depending on the type of wood, such as Thnong, Neang Nuon, and Sokrom. Also added.
Prices start at $ 1,000 and include a plow and a rake. In addition, there are trusses, hammers, baskets, hats, palm leaves and bamboo tubes for customers to display on the cart to enhance the aesthetics.
According to history books, ox carts were also used by the Khmer people in India since the first century AD, during the Phnom Kingdom period. In Pre-Angkor and Angkor, the Khmer ox cart played an important role in the field of social affairs and religious beliefs as well. According to the carvings on the temples of Angkor Wat in the 12th century AD, it is clear that the chariot on which Suryavarman II was seated was a symbol of the sun, the sun, his dynasty.
The ox cart and the horse cart were used by the Khmer people during the reign of Jayavarman VII. This is through sculptures at Bayon temple. Charity donates more than 40 tons of food to the Ministry of Interior for distribution to poor families in Kovid 19
(Phnom Penh): To help alleviate the hardships of the people in the situation of Kovid 19, Roththari Charity Organization donated 20 tons of Angkor, 1 ton of salt, 100 tons of fish sauce, 1 ton of sugar to the Ministry of Interior to distribute to the poor .
The handover ceremony took place on May 21, 2020 at the General Department of Immigration under the chairmanship of Ms. Chou Bun Eng, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Interior.
Ms. Chou Bun Eng said, "So far, we have received donations in the form of food and other supplies from various organizations, including the Rotary Charity, to be distributed to our Cambodian workers. "Livelihoods are scarce, especially losing jobs from Thailand."
The Secretary of State said that in the past, the Ministry has distributed all these donations to the target provinces, going to the community to meet with people in need, especially with people returning from abroad.
In addition, she continued, "Next week, we will visit Kampong Chhnang and Battambang, and will continue to help people who cheated brokers abroad, such as: Kratie, Kampong Thom, Tbong Khmum, Kampong Cham, Battambang, Banteay Meanchey, Kampong Chhnang, Siem Reap and other provinces." Others who are facing great difficulties as well.
She continued, "For the budget as well as the materials donated by the humanitarian organization, we also have a budget package from the Royal Government of Cambodia to help alleviate the hardships of the most vulnerable and vulnerable families in the situation of Kovid 19. This.
In addition to meeting and giving gifts to the needy, we will find out and provide solutions to our people who are vulnerable to various risks, especially wanting to return to work in Thailand, Thailand has not yet opened the door.
On that occasion, the Secretary of State of the Ministry of Interior called on all people who wish to work in the country, please stop, it is not a good time, because in those countries, there is a continuing epidemic of Kovid 19 .
At the same time, the Secretary of State of the Ministry of Interior also thanked the companies and humanitarian organizations, especially the Rotary Club, which has always been actively involved in joining the Royal Government to reduce poverty in Cambodia and fight Kovid 19.
Please be informed that this is not the first time that the "Rotary Club" team has brought materials and food to distribute to the most vulnerable and vulnerable people in Cambodia. In the past, it has also brought medical equipment for the fight against Kovid 19 in the front line doctors of major hospitals such as the Khmer-Soviet Friendship Hospital and provincial referral hospitals.
