WASHINGTON (Reuters) - US President Donald Trump on Thursday announced the withdrawal of the United States from another treaty with Russia and more than 30 other countries. This is according to the release by CGTN on Friday, May 22, 2020
Following Trump's announcement, other signatories expressed regret and called on Washington to reconsider. Russia has said it is under official confirmation from the United States over its withdrawal because Trump's announcement alone is not enough. The US Department of Defense has said it will file a formal withdrawal request soon.
The Open Skies Treaty was established in 2002. It allows one country's military to send reconnaissance aircraft into another airspace every year, observing the territory below, collecting data and capturing various images of that country's bases and military activities. The idea of the treaty is that the more rival nations can know about each other's military might, the lower the chances of a conflict erupting.
